(has been rapid of late years, and has led to much legislative action) Tj T*
(in all civilized countries. The present is a fitting time, therefore,) Tj T*
(to take stock of the general position. We have both, in different) Tj T*
(ways, been occupied with the subject for several years past, the) Tj T*
(one administratively, and the other experimentally, in addition to) Tj T*
(the practical knowledge gained by examining weekly over two hundred) Tj T*
(lead-workers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The present treatise takes account mainly of our own persona) Tj T*
(experience, and of work done in this country, especially by members) Tj T*
(of the Factory Department of the Home Office, and certifying and) Tj T*
(appointed surgeons carrying out periodical medical examinations in lead) Tj T*
(factories. The book, however, has no official sanction.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We are familiar with the immense field of Continental literature) Tj T*
(bearing on legislation against lead poisoning, but have considered any) Tj T*
(detailed reference to this outside the scope of our book, except in) Tj T*
(regard to the medical aspects of the disease.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Most of the preventive measures mentioned are enforced under) Tj T*
(regulations or special rules applying to the various industries) Tj T*
(or under powers conferred by the Factory and Workshops Act, 1901.) Tj T*
(Occasionally, however, where, in the present state of knowledge,) Tj T*
(particular processes are not amenable to the measures ordinarily) Tj T*
(applied, we have suggested other possible lines on which the dangers) Tj T*
(may be met. We have not reprinted these regulations and special rules,) Tj T*
(as anyone consulting this book is sure to have access to them in the) Tj T*
(various works published on the Factory Acts.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The practical value of the experimental inquiry described in Chapter) Tj T*
(VI., and the light it seems to throw on much that has been difficult to) Tj T*
(understand in the causation of lead poisoning, has led us to give the) Tj T*
(results in detail.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One of us \(K. W. G.\) is responsible for Chapters I., III., and V. to) Tj T*
(XI., and the other \(T. M. L.\) for Chapters II. and XII. to XVII.;) Tj T*
(but the subject-matter in all \(except Chapter VI., which is the work) Tj T*
(entirely of K. W. G.\) has been worked upon by both.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Our thanks are due to the Sturtevant Engineering Co., Ltd., London;) Tj T*
(Messrs. Davidson and Co., Ltd., Belfast; the Zephyr Ventilating Co.,) Tj T*
(Bristol; and Messrs. Enthoven and Sons, Ltd., Limehouse, for kindly) Tj T*
(supplying us with drawings and photographs.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  _September, 1912._) Tj T*
