() Tj T*
(CHAPTER I) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(HISTORICAL--CHEMISTRY OF LEAD) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The use of lead for various industrial processes and for painting was) Tj T*
(well known to the ancients. Pliny[1] speaks of white lead, and a method) Tj T*
(of corroding lead in earthen pots with vinegar, sunk into a heap of) Tj T*
(dung, as the means by which white lead was made for paint. Agricola) Tj T*
(mentions three forms of lead--white lead, a compound which was probably) Tj T*
(bismuth, and metallic lead itself. The alchemists were acquainted with) Tj T*
(the metal under the name of ?saturn,? the term signifying the ease) Tj T*
(with which the nobler metals, silver and gold, disappear when added to) Tj T*
(molten lead.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Colic caused by lead was also known in ancient times, and is) Tj T*
(described by Pliny; many other writers refer to it, and Hippocrates) Tj T*
(was apparently acquainted with lead colic. Not until Stockhusen[2],) Tj T*
(however, in 1656, ascribed the colic of lead-miners and smelters to the) Tj T*
(fumes given off from the molten liquid was the definite co-relation) Tj T*
(between lead and so-called ?metallic colic? properly understood,) Tj T*
(and the symptoms directly traced to poisoning from the metal and) Tj T*
(its compounds. Æthius, in the early part of the sixteenth century,) Tj T*
(gave a description of a type of colic called ?bellon,? frequently) Tj T*
(associated with the drinking of certain wines. Tronchin[3], in 1757,) Tj T*
(discovered that many of these wines were able to dissolve the glaze) Tj T*
(of the earthenware vessels in which they were stored, the glaze being) Tj T*
(compounded with litharge.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In our own country, John Hunter[4] describes the frequent incidence of) Tj T*
(?dry bellyache? in the garrison of Jamaica, caused by the consumption) Tj T*
(of rum which had become contaminated with lead. Many other writers in) Tj T*
(ancient and historical books on medicine have written on the causation) Tj T*
(of colic, palsy, and other symptoms, following the ingestion of salts) Tj T*
(of lead; and as the compounds of lead, mainly the acetate or sugar of) Tj T*
(lead, were freely used medicinally, often in large doses, opportunities) Tj T*
(constantly occurred for observing the symptoms produced in susceptible) Tj T*
(persons. It is not to the present purpose to examine the historical) Tj T*
(side of the question of lead poisoning, but those interested will find) Tj T*
(several valuable references in Meillère?s work ?Le Saturnisme?[5].) Tj T*
() Tj T*
