(Lead was used in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries particularly,) Tj T*
(and in the earlier part of the nineteenth, for its action upon the) Tj T*
(blood. In view of experimental evidence of the action of lead on the) Tj T*
(tissues, particularly the blood, this empirical use has interest. Salts) Tj T*
(of lead were found to be hæmostatic, and were therefore used for the) Tj T*
(treatment of ulcers because of the power, notably of lead acetate, of) Tj T*
(coagulating albuminous tissue. It was also used in the treatment of) Tj T*
(fevers, where again it is quite possible that the administration of a) Tj T*
(lead salt, such as an acetate, produced increase in the coagulability) Tj T*
(of the blood. At the same time spasms of colic and other accidents) Tj T*
(followed its use. There is practically no disease to which the human) Tj T*
(body is subject which was not treated by lead in some form or another.) Tj T*
(Lead, with the addition of arsenic, was given for malaria, while its) Tj T*
(use in phthisis was also common. The present use of diachylon plaster) Tj T*
(is an instance of the continuous use of a salt of lead medicinally, as) Tj T*
(also is the lotion of the British Pharmacop?ia containing opium and) Tj T*
(lead.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE CHEMISTRY OF LEAD.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Physical Properties.=--Lead belongs to the group of heavy metals, and) Tj T*
(occupies a position between bismuth and thorium in the list of the) Tj T*
(atomic weights, the atomic weight being 206·4, and density 11·85. It) Tj T*
(is blue-grey in colour, and its softness and facility to form a mark) Tj T*
(upon paper are well known. Lead melts at a temperature of 325° C., and) Tj T*
(at this temperature a certain \(if negligible\) amount of volatilization) Tj T*
(takes place, which vapour becomes reprecipitated in the form of an) Tj T*
(oxide. Use is made of the volatility of the metal at the higher) Tj T*
(temperatures, 550° C. and upwards, in the oxidation of lead from a) Tj T*
