(=The Action of Water upon Lead.=--The action of water on lead was known) Tj T*
(even to the ancients, Pliny and Galen having written on the subject.) Tj T*
(At times, and under certain conditions, as much as 20 milligrammes per) Tj T*
(litre have been found, as in the Bacup epidemic, and 14 milligrammes) Tj T*
(per litre in the epidemic at Claremont. Bisserie[6] in 1900 made an) Tj T*
(exhaustive inquiry into the action of water upon lead; he gives the) Tj T*
(following conclusions:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. Water and saline solutions attack lead more or less readily when it) Tj T*
(is in combination with another metal, such as solder, copper, bronze,) Tj T*
(iron, or nickel, the result being a hydrated oxide.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. The maximum effect is produced with water slightly acid and with) Tj T*
(solutions of chlorides or nitrates. With these it is not necessary to) Tj T*
(have other metals present, and if the water is thoroughly aerated the) Tj T*
(pure metal is attacked.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. Bicarbonates and carbonic acid exercise by themselves an action) Tj T*
(on wet lead, but the carbonate of lead formed in the process adheres) Tj T*
(firmly to the surface of the metal, and prevents any further action.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. Sulphates act in the same way, but in less degree.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(5. This protective action is much diminished when the water is even) Tj T*
(slightly charged with nitrates or organic material. Pouchet has pointed) Tj T*
(out that lead branch-pipes fixed to iron water-pipes, thus producing an) Tj T*
(?iron-lead couple,? set up definite electro-chemical changes, and tend) Tj T*
