(and by the presence of foodstuffs--\(1\) in the undigested and \(2\) in the) Tj T*
(semidigested condition. In considering the absorption of lead products) Tj T*
(from the gastro-intestinal canal, the normal digestive processes should) Tj T*
(not be lost sight of--that is, the sequence of events which occur) Tj T*
(during digestion of food. On swallowing food, no definite acidity is) Tj T*
(present in the stomach for fifteen to twenty minutes, and even after) Tj T*
(that time the hydrochloric acid is only commencing to be secreted. As) Tj T*
(digestion proceeds, and the whole mass becomes partially dissolved,) Tj T*
(such portions as are in a soluble condition are passed through the) Tj T*
(pyloric opening at intervals, and the whole contents of the stomach do) Tj T*
(not pass straight through the pyloric opening as through an ordinary) Tj T*
(straight drain-pipe. As each mass of food passes onwards through the) Tj T*
(pylorus, it comes into contact in the duodenum with pancreatic juice,) Tj T*
(and with the bile, these alkaline fluids rapidly change the reaction,) Tj T*
(and allow the other ferments, trypsin, etc., to become active. As the) Tj T*
(mass proceeds onwards through the intestine, the succus entericus also) Tj T*
(exerts its function. Finally the fluid contents of the intestine are) Tj T*
(passed onwards through the ileo-cæcal valve. During the passage from) Tj T*
(the pylorus to the ileo-cæcal valve, the reaction of the intestinal) Tj T*
(contents undergoes variations, from an alkaline in the duodenum) Tj T*
(or upper parts of the jejunum, to acid at the ileo-cæcal valve.) Tj T*
(Practically no absorption takes place from the stomach itself; a small) Tj T*
(quantity of water and such highly volatile fluids as alcohol may be) Tj T*
(absorbed, but the main absorption is not commenced until the food) Tj T*
(has left the stomach; in fact, the stomach contains no mechanism for) Tj T*
(food absorption. The work of absorption of the products of digestion) Tj T*
(is carried on actively through the small intestine until finally the) Tj T*
(materials have reached the large intestine through the ileo-cæcal) Tj T*
(valve; water is then mainly absorbed, and albuminous fluids and) Tj T*
(substances in solution to some extent, but the amount of absorption) Tj T*
(which takes place is infinitesimal as compared with that of the small) Tj T*
(intestine.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
