(Susceptibility and immunity to poisoning by lead may be considered,) Tj T*
(according to the type of lead compound absorbed, further in its) Tj T*
(relation to age and sex. All compounds of lead are not poisonous in the) Tj T*
(same degree; the more easily soluble compounds are more poisonous than) Tj T*
(the less soluble. On the other hand, compounds which appear at first) Tj T*
(sight unlikely to produce poisoning may do so; for instance, fritted) Tj T*
(lead or lead silicate, a substance largely used in the potteries as a) Tj T*
(glaze, and manufactured by fusing together litharge and a silicate,) Tj T*
(would appear at first sight to be quite an innocuous substance. Owing) Tj T*
(to its method of preparation, however, it is not a pure compound) Tj T*
(of lead and silica, but contains lead oxide, metallic lead, etc.,) Tj T*
(entangled in its meshes, and experimentally one of us \(K. W. G.\) has) Tj T*
(demonstrated that such a compound may be acted on by the tissues of) Tj T*
(the body, both when injected subcutaneously and even when inhaled, and) Tj T*
(so gradually produce definite symptoms of lead poisoning, but at a) Tj T*
(much slower rate than the more poisonous lead compounds. The fineness) Tj T*
(of division in which the compound of lead exists is another factor) Tj T*
(affecting its poisonous nature; the more finely divided particles) Tj T*
(find their way into the lung more easily than the coarser particles.) Tj T*
