(Notification was first enjoined by Section 29 of the Factory and) Tj T*
(Workshop Act, 1895, which subsequently, on consolidation of the Factory) Tj T*
(Acts, became Section 73 of the Act of 1901. This enactment requires) Tj T*
(every medical practitioner, attending on, or called in to visit, a) Tj T*
(patient whom he believes to be suffering from lead poisoning contracted) Tj T*
(in a factory or workshop, to notify the case forthwith to the Chief) Tj T*
(Inspector of Factories at the Home Office; and a similar obligation) Tj T*
(is imposed on the occupier of a factory or workshop to send written) Tj T*
(notice of every such case to the certifying surgeon and inspector of) Tj T*
(factories for the district. In form there is close similarity between) Tj T*
(this section and that requiring notification under the Infectious) Tj T*
(Diseases \(Notification\) Act; but whereas the symptoms of these diseases) Tj T*
(are, within well-recognized limits, precise, in lead poisoning the) Tj T*
(differential diagnosis has not infrequently to be made from a variety) Tj T*
(of common ailments--headache, anæmia, rheumatism, abdominal pain; and) Tj T*
(there is no precise standard of what constitutes lead poisoning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The notification of the practitioner as a rule gives no information) Tj T*
(beyond the belief that the case is one of lead poisoning. As a matter) Tj T*
(of routine the notification is followed up by an inquiry by the) Tj T*
(certifying surgeon and inspector to see whether regulations already in) Tj T*
(force have been infringed in the particular work-place or not, and as) Tj T*
(to how far there may have been contributory negligence on the part of) Tj T*
(the sufferer. The data supplied on the surgeon?s report form the basis) Tj T*
(of the tabulation[1]. Brief explanation is wanted of the method adopted) Tj T*
(in classification. Cases represent all attacks reported within a year,) Tj T*
(and not previously reported within the preceding twelve months, so as) Tj T*
(to make the number of persons and cases in a year the same. Where the) Tj T*
(interval between two reports on the same person was more than twelve) Tj T*
(months, the fresh attack was again included. The number of such second) Tj T*
(reports on persons already included in a return numbered 284 \(4·2 per) Tj T*
(cent.\), and a portion of these certainly, probably not more than 100,) Tj T*
(have been included twice or thrice in the total 6,638 cases. Cases in) Tj T*
(which there was obvious error in diagnosis, or in which the opinion) Tj T*
(of the certifying surgeon was very strongly against the diagnosis) Tj T*
(\(especially when the report had been made in the first instance by) Tj T*
(the occupier alone, and not by a medical practitioner\), were excluded) Tj T*
(from the return. These numbered 458 \(6·8 per cent.\). Others, again,) Tj T*
(where there was a strong element of doubt, but not to be regarded as) Tj T*
(more than a difference of opinion between two medical men, were marked) Tj T*
(doubtful and included. Of these there were 424 \(6·3 per cent.\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The classification of industries was designed to represent the way in) Tj T*
(which the poisoning may be supposed to originate from \(_a_\) lead fumes) Tj T*
(\(1 to 4\), \(_b_\) handling metallic lead \(5 and 6\), \(_c_\) dust from lead) Tj T*
(compounds \(7 to 14\), and \(_d_\) lead paint \(15 to 17\). We attach now) Tj T*
(only slight importance to this attempt to define causation, as it will) Tj T*
(appear from our survey that we regard almost all cases as the result of) Tj T*
(inhalation either of fumes or dust.) Tj T*
