(Table IV. shows the severity of the attacks as stated by the surgeon,) Tj T*
(the number of attack, and the main symptoms. The personal element) Tj T*
(enters into the character of the reports, and symptoms which one) Tj T*
(surgeon might describe as slight another might regard as moderate,) Tj T*
(or even severe. In general, however, ?slight? includes cases of \(1\)) Tj T*
(colic without complication, and of comparatively short duration;) Tj T*
(\(2\) anæmia in adolescence aggravated by employment; and \(3\) either) Tj T*
(of the above with tendency to weakness of the extensors. ?Moderate?) Tj T*
(includes \(1\) a combination of colic with anæmia; \(2\) profound anæmia;) Tj T*
(\(3\) partial paralysis; and \(4\) cases in which there is constitutional) Tj T*
(debility. ?Severe? includes \(1\) marked paralysis; \(2\) encephalopathic) Tj T*
(conditions--convulsions, optic neuritis, and mental affections; \(3\)) Tj T*
(grave undermining of the constitution associated with paralysis,) Tj T*
(renal disease, and arterio-sclerosis. The reports are made during) Tj T*
(the attack, and information is not received of the sequelæ which may) Tj T*
(supervene, except in the event of a later report as the result of) Tj T*
(fresh exposure to lead. Number of attack has reference to definite) Tj T*
(occurrence of disability. Transient attacks which have preceded the) Tj T*
(disabling condition have been usually disregarded. It was necessary) Tj T*
(to limit the number of attacks which might be regarded as indicating) Tj T*
(chronic plumbism, and all those included in Column 10 are either) Tj T*
(third attacks or cases of chronic lead poisoning. Among the main) Tj T*
(symptoms, the headings ?Gastric,? ?Paretic,? ?Encephalopathic,? and) Tj T*
(?Rheumatic or Arthralgic,? represent fairly accurately the relative) Tj T*
(incidence of these in cases of lead poisoning in this country; those) Tj T*
(under the headings ?Anæmia? and ?Headache? are useful in comparing) Tj T*
(relative incidence on the two sexes, but they occur, probably, much) Tj T*
(more frequently than the figures would indicate; those under ?Tremor?) Tj T*
(and ?Other? are less valuable. Under ?Other? are included ?Gout,?) Tj T*
(?Nephritis,? or ?Cerebral Hæmorrhage,? so that entry under this head) Tj T*
(indicates chronic, rather than mild, lead poisoning. The conclusions) Tj T*
(from the table are easy to draw, as, in general, the feature which) Tj T*
(causes severity of symptoms to be prominent leaves its mark also on) Tj T*
(?Number of Attack? and ?Main Symptoms.? Thus, in the industries in) Tj T*
(which severe cases exceed the average \(brass, plumbing, printing,) Tj T*
(file-cutting, tinning, glass-cutting, ship-building, paints used in) Tj T*
