() Tj T*
(Attacks generally are most frequent in the first or second year of) Tj T*
(employment. Thus, of 2,195 attacks reported in the four years 1904) Tj T*
(to 1907, as to which sufficient data are given, 898 occurred in the) Tj T*
(first two years of employment, and of these 672 occurred in the first) Tj T*
(year--that is, three-sevenths of all the cases were reported during) Tj T*
(the first two years, and four-sevenths in the whole of the remaining) Tj T*
(years of employment. It is, unfortunately, impossible to say what) Tj T*
(is the proportion of attacks among those employed for any given age) Tj T*
(period. In some factories--as, for example, lead smelting works--the) Tj T*
(average duration of employment is about thirteen years. The length) Tj T*
(of employment preceding an attack was made out from reports on cases) Tj T*
(which occurred in the white lead industry in 1898--a time when a number) Tj T*
(of new workers were taken on to replace the female labour abolished) Tj T*
(in June of that year, and conditions as regards removal of dust were) Tj T*
(entirely different from what they are now. The figures, therefore, can) Tj T*
(only be considered to have bearing upon incidence under almost the) Tj T*
(worst possible circumstances. Of 155 attacks, duration of employment) Tj T*
(was stated to have been less than 1 week in 3, from 1 week to 1 month) Tj T*
(in 8, from 1 to 3 months in 62, from 3 to 6 months in 44, from 6 to 12) Tj T*
(months in 12, and 1 year and over in 26.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Attempt has been made to discredit the value of Section 73 of the) Tj T*
(Factory Act, 1901, on the ground that the proportion of cases in which) Tj T*
(some degree of paralysis is present is very high as compared with the) Tj T*
(extent found by other observers. The points we have laid stress on--\(1\)) Tj T*
(duration of employment, \(2\) varying kinds and amounts of lead dust and) Tj T*
(fumes--are, we believe, quite sufficient to account for, and give value) Tj T*
(to, the figures dealt with. To them should be added another factor,) Tj T*
(though one of less account--namely, the extent to which particular) Tj T*
(muscles are used. In the case of file-cutters, for instance, there is) Tj T*
(no doubt that the cramped position of the left hand holding the chisel,) Tj T*
(and the work thrown on the right in holding the heavy mallet, determine) Tj T*
(the direction of the paralysis, especially on to the muscles of the) Tj T*
(thenar and hyperthenar eminences and of the fingers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is, however, difficulty in deciding whether such entries on) Tj T*
(reports as ?weakness of arms and legs,? ?weakness of arms,? ?muscular) Tj T*
(weakness,? etc., should be interpreted as incipient paralysis.[A]) Tj T*
(With a disease like lead poisoning showing marked tendency to affect) Tj T*
