(The statistical evidence from death certificates published in the) Tj T*
(decennial supplements of the Superintendent of Statistics[2] is of) Tj T*
(significance, not only in enabling comparison to be made between one) Tj T*
(industry and another, in regard to mortality from lead poisoning, but) Tj T*
(also in determining the other causes of death most frequently entered) Tj T*
(on death certificates of lead workers, and therefore, if they are) Tj T*
(in high excess, as compared with male workers generally, they are) Tj T*
(to be ascribed with some degree of certainty to deleterious effects) Tj T*
(of lead on some of the principal organs. Thus, in Table X. a list) Tj T*
(of occupations is given in which the mortality from plumbism in the) Tj T*
(years 1900 to 1902 was double or more than double the standard. It) Tj T*
(represents the mortality which would occur if the male population in) Tj T*
(the particular industry had exactly the same age population as that of) Tj T*
(?all males.? Further, the annual mortality among ?all males? is taken) Tj T*
(as 1,000, and that of males engaged in the several industries is stated) Tj T*
(as a proportion of this. This ?mortality figure? of 1,000 is made up) Tj T*
