(The contention that, because lead workers die from certain diseases) Tj T*
(more frequently than ?all males,? such diseases must be the sequelæ) Tj T*
(of lead poisoning is untenable unless other recognized causes of the) Tj T*
(diseases in question have been excluded. For excess of deaths from) Tj T*
(phthisis and respiratory diseases the conditions of work and exposure) Tj T*
(to inhalation of mineral and metallic dust or vitiation of atmosphere,) Tj T*
(in pottery, spelter, printing works, and file-cutting workshops,) Tj T*
(sufficiently account. The figures, indeed, take no account of this, and) Tj T*
(their value, in some at any rate, is still further diminished by the) Tj T*
(very large number of occupations \(several involving no contact at all) Tj T*
(with lead\) included in the headings. With exception of the strikingly) Tj T*
(greater proportion of deaths among lead-workers from Bright?s disease,) Tj T*
(the figures are too contradictory to draw deductions from as to what) Tj T*
(are ?sequelæ? of lead poisoning. But this figure--160, as compared) Tj T*
(with 35 for all males--is confirmatory evidence, if any were needed,) Tj T*
(that chronic Bright?s disease is a sequela. And, from the pathology of) Tj T*
(lead poisoning, we believe that the granular condition of the kidney) Tj T*
(is due to the sclerotic change brought about in its substance by) Tj T*
(microscopic hæmorrhages. We have very little evidence indeed in man) Tj T*
(that this interstitial change is set up or preceded by an acute tubal) Tj T*
(nephritis. While we do not deny that there may be some parenchymatous) Tj T*
(change associated with lead poisoning, we do not believe that it) Tj T*
(is of the kind which gives rise to the large white kidney, and we) Tj T*
(should therefore exclude such disease as a sequela. But if chronic) Tj T*
(Bright?s disease is admitted, the train of symptoms associated with) Tj T*
(it--notably arterio-sclerotic changes resulting in cerebral hæmorrhage) Tj T*
(and albuminuric retinitis--must be admitted also. Unless it were) Tj T*
(established that granular nephritis were present in a lead-worker) Tj T*
(before commencement of lead employment, we think it would be useless to) Tj T*
(endeavour to prove that the condition was independent of lead, despite) Tj T*
(its comparative frequency as a cause of death apart from employment.) Tj T*
