() Tj T*
(CHAPTER V) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(PATHOLOGY) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The pathology of lead poisoning has formed the subject of scientific) Tj T*
(inquiry from the time that the association of certain pathological) Tj T*
(symptoms was definitely correlated with poisoning by means of the metal) Tj T*
(or its salts.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Acute poisoning, due to accidental swallowing of large doses of lead) Tj T*
(salts or to use of lead salts criminally, generally produces a train of) Tj T*
(symptoms different from those met with in chronic industrial poisoning.) Tj T*
(But it is difficult to understand why so many writers upon the subject) Tj T*
(of lead poisoning should have attempted to draw a hard-and-fast line) Tj T*
(between the pathological symptoms in acute and chronic poisoning.) Tj T*
(This is especially the case when the after-history of cases of acute) Tj T*
(poisoning is traced, for in a large number of instances a case of) Tj T*
(acute poisoning drifts on into a subacute, and finally a chronic,) Tj T*
(stage. All the symptoms of paralysis, encephalopathy, and even kidney) Tj T*
(degeneration, have been described in persons who were first of all the) Tj T*
(subjects of acute poisoning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The direct effect of a lead salt, such as the acetate, upon the mucous) Tj T*
(membrane of the stomach, is a caustic one, and the attention of) Tj T*
(observers seems to have been focussed on what is really a secondary) Tj T*
(effect of the lead salt, and not one intrinsically associated with) Tj T*
(actual poisoning by the metal itself.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A good deal of experimental work has also been performed in one way) Tj T*
(or another--mainly by feeding with, or inoculation of, considerable) Tj T*
(quantities of soluble lead salts--but, with one or two notable) Tj T*
(exceptions, such experiments have not carried the knowledge of the) Tj T*
(true pathology of lead poisoning very much farther. The statement is) Tj T*
(not uncommonly made that no definite correlation exists between the) Tj T*
(symptoms observed in animals and those observed in man, the reason) Tj T*
(being that the massive doses given to animals cannot be similar to,) Tj T*
(nor can they produce results comparable with, the slow intoxication) Tj T*
(taking place in man; although the after-history of the majority of) Tj T*
(cases of acute poisoning shows that the symptoms suffered are generally) Tj T*
(identical with the severer symptoms seen in cases of chronic poisoning) Tj T*
(of industrial origin. One of the chief reasons explaining this) Tj T*
(remarkable point of view arises from the fact that the tissues which) Tj T*
(come into the hands of the pathologist for post-mortem and histological) Tj T*
(examination are as a rule derived from cases of chronic poisoning,) Tj T*
(cases in which the acute symptoms have drifted into the subacute or) Tj T*
(chronic stage, when any minute changes existing in the initial stages) Tj T*
(of the poisoning have long since disappeared, or their significance has) Tj T*
(been so far obscured by secondary changes that the primary lesions are) Tj T*
(lost sight of.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
