132 0 obj
<< /Length 3090 >>
stream
BT /F1 10 Tf 54 738 Td 13 TL
(A critical examination of the very large amount of literature published) Tj T*
(on lead poisoning negatives the idea that acute and chronic poisoning) Tj T*
(differ fundamentally in their pathology, and observers are found) Tj T*
(describing identical pathological lesions resulting from acute or) Tj T*
(chronic industrial poisoning. It is impossible to review the whole of) Tj T*
(the existing literature. Kobert[1], in summing up the general effect of) Tj T*
(lead upon the animal body, makes the following general statement: ?Lead) Tj T*
(affects especially the striped and unstriped muscles, the epithelium of) Tj T*
(the excretory glands, the neuroglia of the central nervous system, and) Tj T*
(is essentially a protoplasmic poison.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We base our knowledge on definite experiments, so arranged that the) Tj T*
(method of exposure was in every way similar to that in lead industries.) Tj T*
(The only point of difference that can be urged against them is one) Tj T*
(of degree; but as the train of symptoms produced was in every way) Tj T*
(comparable with those suffered by man, this objection cannot be) Tj T*
(sustained. The fact that the symptoms develop in a shorter time than) Tj T*
(they do in industrial processes is merely a function of the intensity) Tj T*
(of the poisoning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An attempt will be made first to summarize the literature on the) Tj T*
(pathology of lead poisoning, and, as such literature covers an immense) Tj T*
(amount of ground, to group the pathological findings of various) Tj T*
(observers, as far as possible, under four main headings--namely:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Gastro-intestinal system.) Tj T*
(  Nervous system.) Tj T*
(  Excretory system.) Tj T*
(  Circulatory system.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Gastro-Intestinal.=--As the chief early symptom of all types of) Tj T*
(chronic poisoning is abdominal colic, early investigators turned their) Tj T*
(attention to the pathology in this region, and ascribed the colic to) Tj T*
(various causes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Oliver[2] noted that, in animals poisoned with lead, the intestine was) Tj T*
(found to be irregularly contracted, and ascribed the pain in abdominal) Tj T*
(colic to the irregular contraction of the intestines themselves,) Tj T*
(supposing that the effect of lead was on the muscular tissues. He also) Tj T*
(noted the presence in the intestine, both large and small, of staining) Tj T*
(due to lead, and the considerable amount of lead to be found in the) Tj T*
(large intestine. We have only met with staining by lead in the large) Tj T*
(intestine.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Dixon Mann[3] pointed out that the fæces contain two-thirds of the) Tj T*
(amount of lead taken by the mouth in experimental cases, and considered) Tj T*
