(Although the kidney suffers directly from the effect of circulating) Tj T*
(lead, the amount of lead excreted by the kidney in chronic cases is) Tj T*
(usually small, variable in quantity, and very rarely exceeds more than) Tj T*
(5 milligrammes in the twenty-four hours.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The chemical estimations of the quantity of lead found in the kidney of) Tj T*
(persons who have died of lead poisoning given by different observers,) Tj T*
(vary exceedingly. Even in cases of definite lead poisoning, where there) Tj T*
(can be no reasonable doubt as to the diagnosis, many cases are on) Tj T*
(record where no lead at all has been discovered in the kidney.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is not acute nephritis which is seen in lead poisoning, but the) Tj T*
(chronic cirrhotic variety. This probably takes a very long time to) Tj T*
(develop; indeed, animals kept under the influence of lead for two years) Tj T*
(show very little kidney destruction. It is quite possible that in the) Tj T*
(kidney disease met with in lead-workers the combined effect of alcohol) Tj T*
(with lead is really the causative factor. There is not sufficient) Tj T*
(statistical evidence to make a definite statement on this point, and it) Tj T*
(would only be possible by comparing the records of the autopsies of a) Tj T*
(number of persons working in lead who do not die from lead poisoning,) Tj T*
(and who were non-alcoholic, with a similar number of persons who, in) Tj T*
(addition to their lead absorption, were alcoholic subjects.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is unusual to find blood in the urine, and the condition of the) Tj T*
(kidney does not suggest that it would be present.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Gull and Sutton[42] have described arterio-capillary fibrosis in which) Tj T*
(the intima of the larger vessels became greatly hypertrophied, and) Tj T*
(many of the smaller vessels are practically destroyed by obliterative) Tj T*
(arteritis. The production of arterio-sclerosis, with attendant) Tj T*
(thickening of the vessel walls and with the various symptoms commonly) Tj T*
(associated with arterio-sclerosis, were regarded as secondary symptoms) Tj T*
(of lead poisoning. The action of lead on the vessel walls themselves) Tj T*
(thus independently proved by a large number of observers working at) Tj T*
(different aspects of the problem suggests the pathological change) Tj T*
(in the vessels as the common element in the cause of these diverse) Tj T*
(symptoms of lead poisoning--colic, paralysis, mania. Generally) Tj T*
(speaking, however, the attention of most has been rather focussed on) Tj T*
(the kidney and the degenerative changes occurring in that organ due to) Tj T*
(the irritative action of lead in the process of excretion than on the) Tj T*
(vessels themselves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Lead in the urine is by no means so common nor so definite a symptom) Tj T*
(of lead poisoning as might be supposed, considering the extreme manner) Tj T*
(in which the kidneys suffer in old-standing cases. Of particular) Tj T*
(importance in this respect is the case referred to by Zinn[43], where a) Tj T*
(woman aged thirty-three received 20 grammes of lead acetate in error.) Tj T*
(After the first acute symptoms had passed off, the case drifted on to) Tj T*
