() Tj T*
(It was thought that some light might be thrown on chronic intoxication) Tj T*
(produced by lead salts if direct experiment were made upon animals,) Tj T*
(resembling in the arrangement of such experiments, as far as possible,) Tj T*
(the industrial conditions under which human beings contract lead) Tj T*
(poisoning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The animals chosen for the experiments were cats, as it is a fact of) Tj T*
(common knowledge that it is impossible to keep cats in lead works,) Tj T*
(particularly white-lead, because they rapidly become poisoned if) Tj T*
(allowed to stray about the works. The same holds good in the case of) Tj T*
(dogs.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From the statistics already given in Chapter IV., and from the remarks) Tj T*
(in the chapter on Ætiology, there was no doubt whatever that dust) Tj T*
(played a most important rôle in the production of industrial lead) Tj T*
(poisoning. In attempting, therefore, to copy the industrial conditions,) Tj T*
(it is essential to submit the animals experimented upon to infection) Tj T*
(by means of air in which lead dust is suspended. A large number of) Tj T*
(experiments have been carried out in the first place, by myself[1],) Tj T*
ET
