(and later in conjunction with Dr. Goodbody[2], and another series of) Tj T*
(experiments were also undertaken by myself[3]. Further experiments are) Tj T*
(still in progress in this and other directions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The methods of experiment adopted were as follows:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. =Breathing Experiments=--_First Series._--A. The animals) Tj T*
(experimented upon were placed in a large closed chamber at one end of) Tj T*
(which an electric fan was fitted in such a way that the air was kept) Tj T*
(in constant motion. The lead dust was introduced by means of a funnel) Tj T*
(through the roof in a definite quantity during timed intervals. By) Tj T*
(means of an aspirating jar and a tube inserted into the side, samples) Tj T*
(of air were withdrawn from time to time during the experiments, and) Tj T*
(submitted to chemical analysis to determine the quantity of lead) Tj T*
(circulating in the air. These samples were drawn off at the level of) Tj T*
(the animals? heads. Great care was taken to eliminate any swallowing of) Tj T*
(dust by the animals during the experiments, by protecting their coats) Tj T*
(from the dust and carefully brushing them at the conclusion of each) Tj T*
(exposure.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Second Series._--B. In other experiments a chamber containing two) Tj T*
(separate compartments was constructed, and lead dust suspended in) Tj T*
(air was blown into the two compartments by means of an electric fan) Tj T*
(situated outside. The apparatus was so arranged that the draught of) Tj T*
(air from the fan passed through two separate boxes, in which the lead) Tj T*
(compound under experiment was kept agitated by means of small fans) Tj T*
(situated in the boxes and driven by a second electric motor. In this) Tj T*
(way two different samples of lead were experimented on at one and the) Tj T*
(same time, the air current driving in the dust through the two boxes) Tj T*
(being equal on the two sides; the quantity of dust was therefore) Tj T*
(directly proportional to the compound used. Samples of the dusty air) Tj T*
(were aspirated off and subjected to analysis, as in the first series.) Tj T*
(In this series of experiments the animals were so arranged that only) Tj T*
(their heads projected into the dust chamber during exposure.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. =Feeding Experiments.=--Feeding experiments were carried out by) Tj T*
(mixing a weighed dose of the lead compound experimented with, and) Tj T*
(adding this to a small portion of the animal?s first feed in the) Tj T*
(morning. It was found that unless the lead was well incorporated with) Tj T*
(the animal?s food it would not take the lead in the dry form; and in) Tj T*
(dealing with white lead and other dust, it was necessary to give the) Tj T*
(compound in a similar form to that in which a man would obtain it under) Tj T*
(industrial conditions, which of course precluded the use of a solution.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The amount of lead given by the mouth as a control to the inhalation) Tj T*
(experiments was from seven to ten times the dose which could be taken) Tj T*
(by the animal during its exposure in the cage, and the dose was given) Tj T*
(daily, and not every third day as in the inhalation experiments. All) Tj T*
(the compounds used in the inhalation experiments were given to animals) Tj T*
(by the mouth, the animals? weights being carefully noted.) Tj T*
