() Tj T*
(In a further series of feeding experiments a soluble salt of lead) Tj T*
(was added to the animals? food \(water or milk\), the salt in this case) Tj T*
(being the nitrate. The quantity added was much smaller than in the dust) Tj T*
(experiments. 0·1 gramme was given daily.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. =Inoculation Experiments.=--As a further control to both the) Tj T*
(breathing and feeding experiments, the various lead compounds similar) Tj T*
(to those used in the other experiments were inoculated into animals.) Tj T*
(The insoluble salts gave some difficulty in the technique of injection,) Tj T*
(but by using a large needle, and making the suspension of the material) Tj T*
(in the syringe, the difficulty was overcome. The quantity of material) Tj T*
(inoculated varied; it was calculated in fractions of a gramme per) Tj T*
(kilogramme body weight, the quantity of fluid used being the same in) Tj T*
(all cases--namely, 10 c.c.--and inoculations were made subcutaneously) Tj T*
(and intramuscularly in the muscles of the back after previous shaving.) Tj T*
(In only one case was localized inflammation produced, and this was when) Tj T*
(the acetate was the salt employed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(None of the animals exhibited any signs of discomfort during the) Tj T*
(experiments from the presence of lead dust in the air; once or twice) Tj T*
(sneezing was noticed, but this was an uncommon occurrence. This point) Tj T*
(is of practical importance, as the lead dust contained in the air in) Tj T*
(white-lead and other factories is not of itself irritating to the) Tj T*
(mucous membrane of the lung. The animals subjected to this form of) Tj T*
(experiment were no doubt absorbing much larger doses of lead than are) Tj T*
(persons engaged in the manufacture of lead compounds.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The only ascertainable difference in the ultimate pathological lesions) Tj T*
(produced in animals, whether inhaling large quantities or minimal) Tj T*
(quantities of dust, was the rate at which the poisoning took place. In) Tj T*
(certain experiments it was found that the animals maintained a kind) Tj T*
(of equilibrium, much as do workmen engaged in dusty lead processes.) Tj T*
(It was found, moreover, that some animals showed a certain amount of) Tj T*
(tolerance to the effect of lead dust, in that their weights remained) Tj T*
(almost constant, but an increase in the quantity of lead present in the) Tj T*
(air immediately produced progressive diminution in the body weight; and) Tj T*
(as this diminution in the body weight approached to one-third of the) Tj T*
(animal?s initial weight, so symptoms of chronic poisoning supervened.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In addition to the animals inhaling lead dust over prolonged periods,) Tj T*
(certain other inhalation experiments were made for the determination) Tj T*
(of lead dust in the lung as opposed to the stomach. In the inhalation) Tj T*
(experiments proper, where the animals were exposed to inhalation every) Tj T*
(other day or every third day, for only an hour at a time, the quantity) Tj T*
(of lead present in the air was not very large, and it was thought) Tj T*
(essential to determine if, in exceedingly dusty atmospheres, any) Tj T*
(appreciable amount of lead could be found in the stomach. Ten animals) Tj T*
(were submitted to the inhalation of air heavily charged with various) Tj T*
(types of lead dust. The animal was exposed to the dust for an hour) Tj T*
(and a half to two hours; at the end of this time it was anæsthetized,) Tj T*
