(white lead and other feeding experiments. In one case the lead was) Tj T*
(given mixed with water, in the other mixed with milk. The animal which) Tj T*
(was fed with the nitrate dissolved in water developed encephalopathy,) Tj T*
(whereas the one in which the substance was mixed in milk exhibited no) Tj T*
(signs, though fed for a similar period. Both the animals increased in) Tj T*
(weight, which is an unusual effect in experimental lead poisoning.) Tj T*
(The question of the addition of milk, which apparently prevented the) Tj T*
(absorption of lead, is of very considerable importance, as it is highly) Tj T*
(probable, as has been pointed out with regard to the precipitation of) Tj T*
(lead by means of organic substances, that the albuminoid substances in) Tj T*
(the milk precipitate the nitrate already in a state of solution; and it) Tj T*
