(CASE 2.--A man who had been employed in an electrical accumulator works) Tj T*
(for a considerable time, and who had had a history of several attacks) Tj T*
(of lead colic and one of lead paresis affecting both hands.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The man?s work in connection with lead ceased from the time of the) Tj T*
(paresis, but some three years subsequently he died with cerebral) Tj T*
(hæmorrhage.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Portions of the organs, brain, kidney, liver, and spleen, were examined) Tj T*
(histologically, stained in the ordinary way with hæmatoxylin and eosin.) Tj T*
(In the brain the same marked microscopical hæmorrhages were observed) Tj T*
(as described in the previous case, and in addition many more areas of) Tj T*
(old fibroid scars, very minute, but apparently corresponding to earlier) Tj T*
(minute hæmorrhages. The kidney showed definite interstitial hæmorrhage) Tj T*
(\(see Plate III., Fig. 2\), as did the liver and spleen.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A portion of the brain was further submitted to chemical examination,) Tj T*
(and 0·0014 gramme of lead was determined as present.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The importance of this confirmatory evidence is undoubted, as the) Tj T*
(presence of the capillary hæmorrhage existing in the tissues of a) Tj T*
(person dying under suspicious circumstances when employed in a lead) Tj T*
(process is confirmed by the chemical determination of lead in the) Tj T*
(tissues.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The following tables, arranged under three headings, give some of the) Tj T*
(experimental results obtained by submitting animals to the effect of) Tj T*
(compounds of lead.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
