(Table XI. gives the inoculation experiments.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The materials used in these experiments were those used in the) Tj T*
(inhalation and feeding experiments. The experiments are also arranged) Tj T*
(in such a manner that each series is a control one of the other.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The amount of substance used for the inoculation gives some rough idea) Tj T*
(of the dose required to produce poisoning in an animal; but even this) Tj T*
(question of dose in absolute quantities, administered hypodermically,) Tj T*
(shows considerable variation in the degree of poisonous effect. The) Tj T*
(first animal in Table XI. was inoculated with acetate, this being one) Tj T*
(of the more soluble lead compounds, and was given it in three small) Tj T*
(doses. The animal received 0·3 gramme per kilogramme of body weight,) Tj T*
(whereas in No. 35 2 grammes of washed frit--that is to say, lead glaze) Tj T*
(formed by fusing together litharge and silica--actually did produce) Tj T*
(symptoms, but of a mild nature. Animal No. 33 only had 0·16 gramme,) Tj T*
(being 0·05 gramme per kilogramme of body weight; and this caused acute) Tj T*
(symptoms. 0·35 gramme of white lead in a 3·500 kilogramme animal) Tj T*
(\(No. 31\) produced no symptoms at all. In the list of the inoculation) Tj T*
(experiments, three animals only exhibited no symptoms--one of these) Tj T*
(\(No. 31\) which was given white lead hypodermically, and Nos. 41 and 42,) Tj T*
(which were inoculated with lead silicate or lead frit, which had been) Tj T*
(previously treated with acetic acid or water.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Several practical points arise from these experiments, notably with) Tj T*
(regard to the frits, as it is seen that a considerable amount of the) Tj T*
(toxic properties contained in frit are removed by washing--most by) Tj T*
(washing with acetic acid and water, but also to some extent by washing) Tj T*
(with hot water alone, showing that in the ordinary production of) Tj T*
(lead frit for pottery purposes a certain amount of lead in a soluble) Tj T*
(condition as regards the body tissues was still present. This is no) Tj T*
(doubt entangled in the true silicates in the forms of oxides, or even) Tj T*
(as carbonate. Further, the toxicity of the lead compounds used may) Tj T*
(certainly be arranged in the order of their solubility with regard to) Tj T*
(the animal tissues, the acetate being the most poisonous, and the frit,) Tj T*
(when washed, the least; but unwashed frit, even in relatively small) Tj T*
(doses, may produce poisoning. This point is still further emphasized) Tj T*
(in Table XIII. Animal No. 42, four months after previous inoculation,) Tj T*
(showed no signs of poisoning. Lead nitrate in water was therefore given) Tj T*
(in quantities of 0·01 gramme per diem; one month later this animal) Tj T*
(developed poisoning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Table XII. deals with the feeding experiments.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In these experiments acetate was given in one case only, and then) Tj T*
(in the form of pills coated with keratin. It is impossible to say,) Tj T*
(however, whether the animal ever received any soluble lead, as on one) Tj T*
(or two occasions the keratin pills passed right through the animal) Tj T*
(without dissolving. On the other hand, feeding with nitrate of lead) Tj T*
(in water produced symptoms, but when the nitrate was given in milk no) Tj T*
(symptoms appeared. It will be noticed it took a cat four months to) Tj T*
(show any signs of poisoning taking 0·1 gramme per day; whereas the) Tj T*
