(animal receiving subcutaneous doses of 0·16 gramme of acetate showed) Tj T*
(paralysis in fifteen days, and in twenty-two days was so ill that it) Tj T*
(had to be destroyed under an anæsthetic. The same relationship in) Tj T*
(time also obtains in the case of the animals fed on dry white lead.) Tj T*
(In practically no instance did definite or severe poisoning follow) Tj T*
(the feeding on dry white lead alone, even when quite large quantities) Tj T*
(were taken. On reference to the inoculation experiments of Table XI.,) Tj T*
(it will be seen that the inoculation of 2 grammes of dry white lead) Tj T*
(produced definite symptoms, although the feeding cats had an amount) Tj T*
(very largely in excess of this. The only animals fed on white lead or) Tj T*
(frit exhibiting signs of lead poisoning were those which were given) Tj T*
(alcohol in addition to the lead compound.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A comparison of the results given in Tables XI. and XII. shows that) Tj T*
(animals which received lead compounds subcutaneously suffered much more) Tj T*
(than the animals which received the lead via the gastro-intestinal) Tj T*
(canal, even when the doses given via the mouth were exceedingly large.) Tj T*
(It will follow, then, that the actual contact with the more intimate) Tj T*
(fluids of the body, rather than the digestive juices, determines the) Tj T*
(solubility and general distribution of the lead compound in the body.) Tj T*
(This is confirmed by a recent paper by Straub[6].) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The animals experimented upon by feeding were kept in the laboratory) Tj T*
(under the same conditions as the inhalation animals, but were so) Tj T*
(placed that under no circumstance could they obtain any lead dust) Tj T*
(by inhalation. These animals were used as control to the breathing) Tj T*
(experiments, the substance fed to the animals being in all cases the) Tj T*
(same substance as was used for the various inhalation experiments; but) Tj T*
(in addition a certain number of the animals were given alcohol, which) Tj T*
(are referred to in Table XII. Alcohol was also given to animal No. 6 in) Tj T*
(the inhalation series.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The animals fed with lead were fed with the same compound which was) Tj T*
(used for the inhalation experiments, 0·4 to 1 gramme being given daily;) Tj T*
(so that during the period these animals were exposed to lead dust the) Tj T*
(other animals were taking the same compound via the intestinal canal,) Tj T*
(but in much larger quantities, and yet they exhibited no signs of lead) Tj T*
(poisoning.) Tj T*
