(It will be seen immediately, on comparing Tables XI. and XII. with) Tj T*
(Table XIII., that the rate of poisoning by means of dust is greatly in) Tj T*
(excess of the rate of poisoning by feeding, even where poisoning by) Tj T*
(feeding actually occurred. Also that the amount of dust present in the) Tj T*
(air inhaled shows a marked correlation with the date of appearance of) Tj T*
(the first symptoms of poisoning, and that where the quantity of dust is) Tj T*
(very much reduced the poisoning was delayed longer than might have been) Tj T*
(expected, and that when poisoning did appear the symptoms were much) Tj T*
(less pronounced than with the more dusty atmospheres; and this although) Tj T*
(the quantity of lead obtained would be relatively the same over the) Tj T*
(range of time the animals were exposed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The knowledge gained in dealing with industrial poisoning clinically) Tj T*
(receives very strong corroboration from these inhalation experiments,) Tj T*
(for it is a well-known fact that many persons engaged in dusty trades) Tj T*
(exhibit a species of immunity to lead poisoning. It is true that some) Tj T*
(susceptible persons, as has already been pointed out, very rapidly) Tj T*
(show signs of poisoning, even with a dosage producing no effect in) Tj T*
(other persons working under similar conditions; and it is highly) Tj T*
(probable that these persons have arrived at a species of equilibrium by) Tj T*
(which they are able to excrete the lead ingested, and so prevent any) Tj T*
(accumulation and general damage to their tissues. Directly, however,) Tj T*
(the dosage is increased, signs of poisoning come on, as in the case) Tj T*
(of animals Nos. 10 and 11. For some seventy to eighty days little or) Tj T*
(no sign of poisoning was seen with the small dosage commenced with. At) Tj T*
(the end of this time, as no symptoms appeared, the quantity of lead in) Tj T*
(the air was increased, with the result that poisoning rapidly became) Tj T*
(manifest.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(We have also in these inhalation experiments, in Cases 21 and 22,) Tj T*
