(The experimental work therefore carries us very considerably forward) Tj T*
(in correlating the symptomatology and pathology of lead poisoning. The) Tj T*
(symptoms produced in susceptible animals by the actual inoculation) Tj T*
(of a lead compound differ only in degree and rapidity of onset from) Tj T*
(those produced in animals submitted to inhalation with similar) Tj T*
(compounds. Feeding, on the other hand--that is, ingestion by way) Tj T*
(of the gastro-intestinal canal--even in large quantities, did not) Tj T*
(produce poisoning to any great extent, except when some material such) Tj T*
(as alcohol was added, thereby breaking down the animal?s resistance.) Tj T*
(Another interesting fact is given--that if lead is taken by the mouth) Tj T*
(in addition to milk a great deal of the poisonous effect is got rid) Tj T*
(of; thus of two animals--Nos. 46 and 47--which received lead nitrate) Tj T*
(in their food, the one in water and the other in milk, the one) Tj T*
(which received it in milk showed no effects even after four months?) Tj T*
(experiment, whereas at the end of four months the animal which was) Tj T*
(receiving the compound in its water died. This brings out a point) Tj T*
(already insisted upon--namely, that in all lead factories it is highly) Tj T*
(important that no work should be undertaken first thing in the morning,) Tj T*
(before the workers have had a proper meal, and that in the absence of) Tj T*
(a proper meal milk is the best substitute. It is highly probable that) Tj T*
(the soluble lead salt becomes united in some form of albuminate which) Tj T*
(is dealt with later, and perhaps turned into a sulphide and excreted) Tj T*
(without absorption. There is no possible doubt, from the large series) Tj T*
(of experiments which I have performed, that lead inhaled is far more) Tj T*
(poisonous than when absorbed in any other way; further, that the) Tj T*
(amount of poisoning produced differs somewhat according to the type of) Tj T*
(compound inhaled, and the experiments, moreover, give some suggestion) Tj T*
(as to the dose which is likely to produce poisoning. It is seen, where) Tj T*
(the animal is inoculated with white lead, the dose required to produce) Tj T*
(symptoms is below 1 gramme per kilogramme of body weight, but above 0·2) Tj T*
(gramme per kilogramme of body weight. In feeding, 0·8 gramme, and even) Tj T*
(1 gramme, per diem for eighteen months produces no effect, although the) Tj T*
(same quantity plus an excess of alcohol rapidly produces the disease.) Tj T*
(On the other hand, as small a dose as 0·1 gramme of nitrate of lead) Tj T*
(given in water for four months produced death.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Turning to the inhalation experiments, the quantity of dust breathed) Tj T*
(when as high as 0·0007 gramme per litre produced symptoms after only) Tj T*
(twelve inhalations for a period of about thirty-seven days; whereas) Tj T*
(when the dose was reduced to 0·0001 gramme per diem the time required) Tj T*
(to produce symptoms of poisoning was 120 days; in fact, this last dose) Tj T*
(\(0·0001\) for the animal under experiment was almost the lower limit, as) Tj T*
(this animal showed an almost steady line of weight for a considerable) Tj T*
(time, the weight remaining up for the first hundred days, a slight) Tj T*
(variation taking place from week to week until a progressive diminution) Tj T*
(set in.) Tj T*
