(=Further Experiments relating to Lead Poisoning amongst Painters.=--A) Tj T*
(series of further experiments were made, with particular reference to) Tj T*
(lead poisoning exhibited by painters; and as these experiments and) Tj T*
(their results could not have been undertaken without the previous) Tj T*
(knowledge gained of the pathology of lead poisoning due to the) Tj T*
(inhalation of particles of dust floating in the air, their discussion) Tj T*
(has been reserved until the previous section had been dealt with.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It has been supposed by some that surfaces painted with lead paint) Tj T*
(give off certain emanations containing the metal lead as an organic) Tj T*
(compound. As the incidence of lead poisoning amongst painters is) Tj T*
(exceedingly high, as far as any statistical evidence can be obtained) Tj T*
(\(see p. 48\), it would seem that the painter is peculiarly exposed to) Tj T*
(infection by lead dust; and if, in addition, organic compounds of lead) Tj T*
(were given off, he would be still more liable to lead poisoning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Two methods of experiment were used:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. The exposure of animals to the fumes given off from freshly painted) Tj T*
(surfaces, the paints used being compounded with white lead, lead) Tj T*
(sulphate, zinc sulphide, and zinc oxide.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Animals were exposed in a cage similar to that used in the inhalation) Tj T*
(experiments previously described, but, instead of blowing in the) Tj T*
(contaminated air, the cages were so arranged that boards freshly) Tj T*
(painted with the special paint under experiment were introduced into) Tj T*
(the cage daily, the animals remaining the whole time in the chamber.) Tj T*
(Special precautions were taken with regard to ventilation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. An animal was placed in a chamber, and the compound to be tested was) Tj T*
(heated electrically by means of a coil surrounding the glass tube in) Tj T*
(which the compound was placed. The current was regulated by means of) Tj T*
(resistances, so that the thermo-couple and galvanometer gave a constant) Tj T*
(reading of 59° C. Air was constantly passed through the tube over the) Tj T*
(heated substance and into the animal?s cage, which was efficiently) Tj T*
(ventilated. In this way any emanations which were given off from the) Tj T*
(normal room temperature or up to 59° C. were carried over into the) Tj T*
(animal?s cage, and there breathed. The apparatus was so arranged that) Tj T*
(the heating coil extended close to the point of delivery into the cage.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The result of these experiments showed that the animals confined in) Tj T*
(cages and exposed to freshly painted surfaces, where the paint used) Tj T*
(was white lead, zinc oxide, or lead sulphate, very soon showed signs) Tj T*
(of poisoning, and they became emaciated and suffered from recurrent) Tj T*
(attacks of salivation. The animals exposed in the cages in which air) Tj T*
(was passed over either white lead paste, zinc paste, or lead sulphate) Tj T*
(paste, showed no signs of illness, although kept in the cage and) Tj T*
(subjected to the inhalation of any fumes which might be given off for) Tj T*
(three months, spending the whole of the day in the cage, but being) Tj T*
(removed during the night to separate cages.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
