(CHAPTER VII) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(SYMPTOMATOLOGY AND DIAGNOSIS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Acute Poisoning.=--Acute poisoning by lead is not common. Industrially) Tj T*
(it hardly ever occurs. Zinn[1] states that, out of 200 cases of) Tj T*
(industrial lead poisoning in his clinic in Berlin, only one was to be) Tj T*
(regarded as an acute case. In most instances the poisoning is due to) Tj T*
(swallowing some compound of lead, either as an abortifacient or to) Tj T*
(commit suicide.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The pathology and symptoms of such acute lead poisoning depend in) Tj T*
(the first place upon the nature of the salt of lead swallowed, as,) Tj T*
(for instance, after swallowing sugar of lead a burning taste is) Tj T*
(complained of, with acute gastric pain, generally coming on within an) Tj T*
(hour of taking the poison, salivation, metallic taste in the mouth,) Tj T*
(acute hiccough, and griping pain in the abdomen. The mouth is stained) Tj T*
(a whitish-grey. Later there is a great fall in blood-pressure, the) Tj T*
(skin becomes moist, or a cold sweat may appear. The respiration and) Tj T*
(pulse drop; finally comes vertigo, acute headache, coldness of the) Tj T*
(extremities, anæsthesia, and death in one or two days, or the case) Tj T*
(passes on to one of chronic poisoning. If the patient survives for) Tj T*
ET
