(of the mucous membrane. The large intestine is generally stained) Tj T*
(darkly, from a light brown to a deepish black; this staining may) Tj T*
(not appear until quite low down in the intestine. There is hyperæmia) Tj T*
(of the liver, much engorgement of the vessels in the mesentery, the) Tj T*
(kidney, and the brain. The rest of the intestinal viscera show signs) Tj T*
(of engorgement. Fluid may be present in the peritoneal cavity, and) Tj T*
(occasionally in the other serous cavities.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The histological examination of the various organs exhibits the) Tj T*
(same microscopical hæmorrhages as are found in the cases of chronic) Tj T*
(poisoning to be described later.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Although acute lead poisoning is rare in industrial experience, it may) Tj T*
(occur from time to time. Several cases are on record where a workman) Tj T*
(developed an acute attack of poisoning as a result of immersion in a) Tj T*
(white lead beck; another case is described which followed immersion in) Tj T*
(a tank of solution of lead acetate.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(An accident of this description may conceivably occur; the treatment) Tj T*
(of such a case should be energetic, as the poisoning is chiefly due) Tj T*
(to lead swallowed. An emetic should be given, followed by sublimed) Tj T*
(sulphur, or, better still, the stomach washed out with dilute hydrogen) Tj T*
(sulphide water slightly acidulated with sulphuric acid, so as to) Tj T*
(change any lead present in the stomach into the least soluble form.) Tj T*
(A brisk purge should be given, and the patient encouraged to drink) Tj T*
(considerable quantities of lemonade containing sodium or potassium) Tj T*
(citrate. Alcohol, even during collapse, should be avoided; a hypodermic) Tj T*
(injection of strychnine is preferable. It must be borne in mind that) Tj T*
(lead is absorbed in the upper part of the intestine, and only in a) Tj T*
(minute degree, if at all, from the stomach; it is re-excreted mainly) Tj T*
(by the large bowel and by the urine; to some extent also by the sweat) Tj T*
(and saliva. Treatment is therefore directed towards \(_a_\) forming an) Tj T*
(insoluble compound as far as possible; \(_b_\) promoting the elimination) Tj T*
(of the poison; \(_c_\) placing as little work as possible upon the) Tj T*
(tissues most affected. Only milk should be given as food for two to) Tj T*
(three days.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The diagnosis of lead poisoning is not in itself of any great) Tj T*
(difficulty where any one of the classical symptoms of lead poisoning is) Tj T*
(present, such as lead colic, paresis, or the characteristic lead anæmia) Tj T*
