(or cachexia. On the other hand, the premonitory symptoms of poisoning,) Tj T*
(as seen by a club doctor, particularly in persons engaged in industrial) Tj T*
(processes, are more difficult to determine; but for the appointed) Tj T*
(surgeon, who has an opportunity of watching them from week to week,) Tj T*
(the gradual development of anæmia, extensor weakness, and other early) Tj T*
(symptoms, should give no difficulty. The clinical diagnosis requires) Tj T*
(to be made earlier by the appointed surgeon than by the general) Tj T*
(practitioner; for the appointed surgeon?s duty in a white lead works is) Tj T*
(not only to treat lead poisoning when it is once established, but, by) Tj T*
(carefully noting premonitory signs, to avoid the development of actual) Tj T*
(symptoms in susceptible persons. It is convenient, therefore, to divide) Tj T*
(the diagnosis of lead poisoning, from the clinical standpoint, into two) Tj T*
(divisions--incipient and pronounced. Such incipient changes are for) Tj T*
(the most part noticed amongst lead-workers, and in many cases are more) Tj T*
(strictly signs of lead absorption than signs of lead poisoning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The earliest symptoms of poisoning are found in the vascular system,) Tj T*
(and the curious pallor of the face in persons who have worked in lead) Tj T*
(for a considerable period is often pronounced, although the conjunctiva) Tj T*
(may not show such a diminution in colour as might be expected from the) Tj T*
(facial change, while the actual determination of hæmoglobin may be) Tj T*
(almost normal. In addition to this, a person of fresh colour working in) Tj T*
(lead, if susceptible, very quickly loses his florid appearance, often) Tj T*
(heightened by the colour of the face only remaining on the cheek-bones) Tj T*
(as a hectic flush. Such a person will also show diminution in the) Tj T*
(colour of the conjunctival vessels, and invariably a distinct yellowish) Tj T*
(appearance of his sclerotics, due to the pigmentation of that tissue by) Tj T*
(broken-down blood-pigment. The yellow colour of the eyes is definite) Tj T*
(evidence that blood-destruction is in progress.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Following on the anæmia, or, more strictly speaking, the pallor of) Tj T*
(the face, a well-marked wasting of the subcutaneous fat takes place.) Tj T*
(In animals poisoned with lead in small or large doses--particularly) Tj T*
(in small doses given over a considerable period--this wasting of all) Tj T*
(subcutaneous and other fat is a very marked feature, so much so that) Tj T*
(practically no kidney, mesenteric or abdominal fat is to be found.) Tj T*
(The fat is lost in a greater proportion than other body tissues. In) Tj T*
