() Tj T*
(=Symptomatology and Diagnosis= \(_Continued_\)--=Excretion of Lead.=--The) Tj T*
(two chief channels for excretion of lead are the urine and the fæces,) Tj T*
(while some include the saliva and the sweat.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the case of the sweat there is not much evidence, but a few) Tj T*
(observers, mainly French, claim to have discovered traces of lead in) Tj T*
(the skin of lead-workers. In such a case, however, it is exceedingly) Tj T*
(difficult to eliminate the question of surface contamination; and) Tj T*
(although brisk peripheral circulation and transudation may possibly) Tj T*
(carry off a certain amount of lead, the chance of this is highly) Tj T*
(improbable.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There seems rather more evidence that the salivary glands may eliminate) Tj T*
(lead, as a number of other substances are undoubtedly passed in this) Tj T*
(way. Mercury certainly undergoes excretion through the salivary) Tj T*
(glands and the mucous glands of the mouth, and it is therefore not) Tj T*
(improbable that a metal so closely related in its chemical, and) Tj T*
(perhaps physiological, relations may be excreted in a similar fashion.) Tj T*
(Meillère[1] cites three instances of parotitis which were considered to) Tj T*
(be of lead origin, and further quotes an instance where, in chemical) Tj T*
(examination of the salivary glands after death, lead was found in small) Tj T*
(quantities.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Chronic parotitis is not infrequently cited as a symptom in cases) Tj T*
(of reported industrial lead poisoning, and may owe its origin to) Tj T*
(impairment of the salivary gland by the passage of the metal. Chronic) Tj T*
(parotitis, or even tenderness of the parotid glands, does not occur) Tj T*
(frequently among lead-workers with symptoms of lead absorption.) Tj T*
