(be detected on applying suitable tests.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In well-established cases of chronic poisoning, albuminuria as a rule) Tj T*
(is found, together with certain alterations in the other constituents) Tj T*
(of the urine, such alteration frequently making its appearance before) Tj T*
(the definite onset of albuminuria. Further, the changes in the eye,) Tj T*
(referred to under a special heading, have been frequently described as) Tj T*
(albuminuric retinitis of lead origin, it being true that eye changes) Tj T*
(are often associated with chronic changes in the kidney.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In acute poisoning lead is generally found in the urine, but in chronic) Tj T*
(poisoning it is by no means a common occurrence. From time to time) Tj T*
(small quantities are excreted, and in the chemical analysis made of) Tj T*
(the kidneys in cases of fatal lead poisoning a certain amount of lead) Tj T*
(has frequently been noted. Wynter Blyth[2] found in the kidneys of two) Tj T*
(white lead workers a total of 0·003 gramme. Peyrusson and Pillault[3],) Tj T*
(quoted by Meillère, found a similar quantity, 0·003 gramme, while in) Tj T*
(experimental animals Meillère himself found considerably less, only) Tj T*
(0·0001 gramme; Stevenson, in a case reported by Newton Pitt[4], 0·0086) Tj T*
