(per cent. of lead in the cæcum and colon. Notwithstanding a small) Tj T*
(quantity of lead which may be determined by chemical methods as present) Tj T*
(in the urine or the kidney, very definite nephritis is set up in these) Tj T*
(organs, obviously due to the irritative effect of the metallic poison.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=The Kidneys.=--Kidney disease of several types has been described) Tj T*
(as associated with poisoning by lead, particularly with chronic lead) Tj T*
(poisoning, where large quantities of a soluble lead salt have been) Tj T*
(ingested. Very considerable strain is thrown upon the kidney, with the) Tj T*
(result that the lead salts themselves are passed through; but, as has) Tj T*
(been pointed out when dealing with acute poisoning, the passage of lead) Tj T*
(through the kidneys does not continue for any considerable time, and in) Tj T*
(lead poisoning of an industrial and chronic nature no lead at all has) Tj T*
(been found in the urine in undoubted cases. Even when present, it may) Tj T*
(be difficult to detect unless the electrolytic method is used \(see p.) Tj T*
(174\). At the same time kidney disease undoubtedly does occur in a very) Tj T*
(large number of workers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(All heavy metals, of which silver, mercury, iron, zinc, and finally) Tj T*
(lead, may be quoted as examples, appear to be eliminated by the kidney) Tj T*
(when they are present in the body in toxic doses, and often when in) Tj T*
(small non-toxic doses, but in the latter case to a greater extent) Tj T*
(through the bowel than through the kidney. The lead circulating in the) Tj T*
(blood, in common with other heavy metals, may be found chemically in) Tj T*
(the kidney, but the quantity recovered is not as large as one would) Tj T*
(expect from the considerable amount of inflammation often present.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the experiments on animals, subjected to poisoning over considerable) Tj T*
(periods, the condition of the kidney in every instance showed distinct) Tj T*
(histological changes; and the longer such animals had been subjected) Tj T*
(to the poisonous effects of the metal, the more advanced were the) Tj T*
(signs of degeneration in its structure. In the earliest cases the) Tj T*
(disease partook much more of the nature of an interstitial nephritis,) Tj T*
(and it was in the later and more chronic stages only that changes) Tj T*
(in the glomeruli and fibroid degeneration were to be found, but in) Tj T*
(even these earliest cases of poisoning definite minute interstitial) Tj T*
(hæmorrhages were to be found scattered about the kidney. These minute) Tj T*
(hæmorrhages did not cause symptoms of hæmaturia, as in none of the) Tj T*
(experimental animals was bloody urine observed. On the other hand,) Tj T*
(besides definite small areas of hæmorrhage, patches were discoverable,) Tj T*
(indicative of hæmorrhage which had undergone fibroid change. Even in) Tj T*
(the illustrations given by Glibert[5], there appears to be evidence) Tj T*
(that hæmorrhage had taken place and had undergone fibroid degeneration,) Tj T*
(and there is very little doubt that in cases of kidney disease, the) Tj T*
(preliminary action of the poison determines small local yieldings) Tj T*
(of the vessel walls, with leakage, often hardly amounting to true) Tj T*
(hæmorrhage, at such spots. There is nothing opposed to the theory in) Tj T*
(the findings of other observers; in fact, if the preliminary gross) Tj T*
(effect be leakage of the description given, all the other lesions) Tj T*
(described by various observers follow as a corollary.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
