(In the kidney, as in the other regions of the body, the venioles rather) Tj T*
(than the arterioles appear to be the preliminary site of destruction,) Tj T*
(and microscopical observation of the sections leads to the view that) Tj T*
(the intima of the vessels and not the media or the muscular coat is) Tj T*
(the one affected in the first place. Capillary hæmorrhages under) Tj T*
(these conditions are easier to understand than if the arterioles) Tj T*
(themselves or their muscular or middle coats were primarily affected.) Tj T*
(As degenerative changes progress, the whole of the vessel--external and) Tj T*
(middle coats and intima--undergoes change, ultimately resulting in the) Tj T*
(extreme narrowing and consequent blocking of the vessels themselves.) Tj T*
(Further shrinkage taking place in this area produces the shrunken) Tj T*
(sclerosed kidney.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Zinc in the form of oxide behaves in very much the same way upon the) Tj T*
(kidney as does lead. An experimental animal, which was given 0·2 gramme) Tj T*
(of zinc oxide per kilogramme body weight by hypodermic injection in) Tj T*
(the muscles of the back, died in fifteen days, and the kidneys showed) Tj T*
(extensive hæmorrhages--not merely the minute and capillary hæmorrhages) Tj T*
(found in lead poisoning, but hæmorrhages extending right through from) Tj T*
(the cortex.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Clinically, kidney disease, unless albumin be detected in the urine,) Tj T*
(is not a prominent symptom during the progress of an attack of chronic) Tj T*
(lead poisoning, and is to be regarded as a late symptom, developing as) Tj T*
(the result of long-continued irritation. The difficulty of eliminating) Tj T*
(alcoholic complication has been discussed, and there are no specific) Tj T*
(symptoms or post-mortem signs which enable one to distinguish alcoholic) Tj T*
(nephritis from the nephritis of lead poisoning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the chapter on Pathology, the effect of alcohol on the kidney was) Tj T*
(cited as a common predisposing cause of kidney disease in lead-workers,) Tj T*
(and the effect of alcoholic excess in the case of a person who is) Tj T*
(already the subject of chronic lead absorption may determine the change) Tj T*
(from absorption to definite poisoning, because of alteration in the) Tj T*
(excreting-power of the kidney. So long as the ratio between ingestion) Tj T*
(and excretion is maintained the balance is kept up, and, although the) Tj T*
(tissues of the body may show signs of a certain amount of degeneration,) Tj T*
(no definite disease is produced; but the gastric irritation and the) Tj T*
(work thrown upon the kidney in removing from the blood large quantities) Tj T*
(of alcohol may be sufficient to alter this absorption-excretory balance) Tj T*
(and determine the attack of poisoning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Acute nephritis is rare, and cannot be regarded as a sequela of chronic) Tj T*
(lead poisoning. Acute nephritis occurring in a lead-worker, with the) Tj T*
(associated symptoms of general ?dema of the face, eyes, hands, feet,) Tj T*
(is of the gravest possible moment, and such a sudden appearance of) Tj T*
(nephritis is almost invariably fatal. In chronic nephritis, to which) Tj T*
(most of the lead cases belong, the usual signs are to be found in) Tj T*
(the urine. Pain is rarely a symptom; and although pain in the back) Tj T*
(is often complained of by lead-workers, examination rarely suggests) Tj T*
(that the backache is of kidney origin, but rather the lumbago type) Tj T*
(associated with chronic constipation. Care, however, must be taken,) Tj T*
