(According to Garrod, etc., the alkalinity of the blood is decreased in) Tj T*
(lead absorption.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The white blood-corpuscles do not show any change in their structure) Tj T*
(by the ordinary methods of staining, but they apparently show, as do) Tj T*
(the red cells, more resistance to injury in spreading blood-films--that) Tj T*
(is to say, the viscosity of the blood, which is apparently increased) Tj T*
(in lead poisoning, is also exhibited by the white cells. In the) Tj T*
(early stages of lead poisoning, more especially in acute lead) Tj T*
(poisoning, distinct leucocytosis may be observed, such a leucocytosis) Tj T*
(showing itself rather in relation to the lymphocytes than to the) Tj T*
(polymorphonuclear cells. In addition, the large mononuclear cells are) Tj T*
(also greatly increased, and a differential blood-count from a case of) Tj T*
(lead poisoning which also shows the presence of basophile granules) Tj T*
(in the cells invariably brings to light a definite increase in the) Tj T*
(percentage number of lymphocytes, and a decrease in the number of) Tj T*
(polymorphonuclears, and this even when the total leucocyte count is) Tj T*
(not outside the ordinary limit of normal variation. On the whole, the) Tj T*
(number of leucocytes present in the blood of persons suffering from) Tj T*
(lead absorption will always be found to tend rather towards the higher) Tj T*
(than the lower limit of normal variation.) Tj T*
