(=Circulatory System.=--A very large number of the symptoms referable to) Tj T*
(chronic and well-defined lead poisoning are referable to circulatory) Tj T*
(lesions, and, as has been elsewhere pointed out, the ultimate nerve) Tj T*
(degeneration occurring in various parts of the body is probably but) Tj T*
(a final symptom of the earlier hæmorrhage which has taken place.) Tj T*
(Certain symptoms are, however, more closely related to the circulation) Tj T*
(than others, and may therefore be more conveniently grouped together) Tj T*
(under the present heading. The smaller changes, many of them connected) Tj T*
(with special organs \(such, for instance, as the eye\) or particular) Tj T*
(regions \(as the mesenteric vessels\), have been already referred to in) Tj T*
(dealing with colic and eye changes. Vaso-motor changes precede the) Tj T*
(actual change in the vessel walls themselves. On the other hand, the) Tj T*
(alterations in the structure of the liver, lung, spleen, and more) Tj T*
(especially the kidney, are secondary to change in the structure of the) Tj T*
(walls of the vessels themselves.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Vaso-motor disturbances may or may not be of nervous origin, although) Tj T*
(the former view is probably correct, and it is also equally possible) Tj T*
