(=Symptomatology and Diagnosis= \(_Continued_\)--=The Nervous) Tj T*
(System.=--The most definite objective symptoms of chronic poisoning) Tj T*
(by means of lead are those of the nervous system. From the time of) Tj T*
(Tanquerel[1] affections of the hands and fingers and the muscles of the) Tj T*
(back have all been well known. Associated with the paralysis are local) Tj T*
(vaso-motor effects, such as cyanosed condition of the skin over the) Tj T*
(paralyzed muscles, cold hands, etc.; whilst later, if the paralysis be) Tj T*
(severe and persists, atrophic changes take place in the skin, muscles,) Tj T*
(bone, whilst definite contracture occurs from unopposed contraction of) Tj T*
(the unaffected muscles. Paralysis will therefore be associated with two) Tj T*
(of the great systems into which the body is divided for the purposes) Tj T*
(of medical and physiological description--namely, the muscular and the) Tj T*
(nervous--and, on account of the similarity of the clinical symptoms of) Tj T*
(lead paralysis, attention has been drawn rather to the nerve changes) Tj T*
(preceding muscular paralysis and degeneration than to other influences) Tj T*
(affecting the nerve inflammation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Lancereaux[2] considered that lead poisoning resulting in paralysis) Tj T*
(takes the form of a gradual impregnation of the nervous tissue with) Tj T*
(lead salts, until such a time as degenerative effects are set up, and) Tj T*
(with it muscular paralysis.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Meillère[3], who has given much attention to the ætiology of lead) Tj T*
(poisoning, as well as to the clinical study of the disease, considers) Tj T*
(that plumbism may be divided into three periods:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(\(_a_\) Impregnation of the tissues of the body, the nervous tissue being) Tj T*
(the chief one affected by lead salts.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(\(_b_\) Retardation of the general oxidation changes of the body,) Tj T*
(resulting in malnutrition and general loss of tone.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(\(_c_\) Establishment of intoxication, with the generalized affections,) Tj T*
(paresis, etc.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If three such periods can be recognized, as no doubt they can, as) Tj T*
(divisions of the time during which lead gradually affects the tissues,) Tj T*
(the symptoms in the more severe cases would be expected to be those) Tj T*
(associated with the more prolonged exposure. This, no doubt, is true to) Tj T*
