(Such selective action is, of course, exceeded by a functional activity) Tj T*
(which brings about the affection of those nerves which supply the) Tj T*
(muscles most used. If, therefore, we judge of paralysis as being due to) Tj T*
(the selective action of lead on certain nerves, we are met at once with) Tj T*
(the objection that the muscles affected do not always correspond to) Tj T*
(such a nerve distribution, and that muscles supplied by other than the) Tj T*
(musculo-spiral nerve are affected by paralysis.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Careful consideration of the chapter on Pathology, and more) Tj T*
(particularly of the histological findings described, in which the) Tj T*
(preliminary action of lead is found to be typically and invariably on) Tj T*
(the blood, setting up degenerative changes microscopical in size and) Tj T*
(limited in area, affecting the vessel walls and producing a yielding of) Tj T*
(the vessel, determining minute microscopical hæmorrhages distributed,) Tj T*
(not necessarily in one position of the body, but all over the body, and) Tj T*
(peculiarly in the case of the cat affecting those muscles called upon) Tj T*
(to perform sudden and violent movement--namely, jumping--enables us to) Tj T*
(regard such microscopical hæmorrhages as an adequate explanation of) Tj T*
(the association of paralysis in muscle groups, functionally related to) Tj T*
(various trades and industrial processes.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It may be argued, and we think with considerable reason, both from) Tj T*
(pathological and clinical findings, that, as muscular exertion is) Tj T*
(apparently associated with the onset of paralysis, particularly in) Tj T*
(those muscles which may be regarded as physically somewhat inadequate) Tj T*
(to the work they have to perform, and that, as the paralysis is) Tj T*
(associated with definite functional groups of muscles, and to a curious) Tj T*
(extent varies according to the trade in which the sufferer is engaged,) Tj T*
(therefore greater stress thrown upon the muscular tissue at some period) Tj T*
(or another during occupation determines the microscopical hæmorrhage in) Tj T*
(the nerve supplying the muscles, or in the muscle itself, so that the) Tj T*
(paralysis affects just such muscles as have an increased strain thrown) Tj T*
(upon them. It does not necessarily follow that the preliminary initial) Tj T*
(hæmorrhage occurring should be a large one--in fact, from the whole of) Tj T*
(the histological history, hæmorrhages are exceedingly minute; neither) Tj T*
(does it follow that it is essential for such hæmorrhages to take place) Tj T*
(in the whole length of the nerve itself; but it is only necessary) Tj T*
(that the finer branches of the nerve should have their venioles) Tj T*
(or arterioles affected, and it is of course in the finer branches) Tj T*
(particularly, as has been pointed out in relation to the venioles, that) Tj T*
(degeneration of the intima of the vessels takes place.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Finally, the effect of early treatment on lead palsy tends to bear out) Tj T*
(this theory. If a case of lead palsy be treated in the early stages,) Tj T*
(the clinical course of the case is good; increased paralysis generally) Tj T*
(takes place in the first week, and, where, perhaps, only two or three) Tj T*
(fingers are involved when the case is first seen, spreads generally) Tj T*
(to other regions within a week, and the whole hand is affected; but) Tj T*
(from this moment onwards improvement takes place on the application of) Tj T*
(suitable treatment, and, if continued, almost invariably results in the) Tj T*
(entire recovery from the paresis.) Tj T*
