(recovered. This man showed the ordinary anterior brachial type, which) Tj T*
(came on suddenly, although he had shown distinct weakness of wrists) Tj T*
(when forcible flexion was performed for nine months previously, but) Tj T*
(there had been no obvious increase in the weakness. He was immediately) Tj T*
(removed from his work, and within seven days paralysis, which at first) Tj T*
(only affected the extensor communis digitorum, had spread to the minimi) Tj T*
(digiti and the extensor indicis, the opponens pollicis being also) Tj T*
(involved on the right side. He was treated from the start with faradic) Tj T*
(current, and was instructed to use the battery himself, which he did) Tj T*
(twice a day for a year. At the end of two months he was sufficiently) Tj T*
(recovered to be given light work, and at the time of taking the) Tj T*
(reactions his wrists have so far recovered their power that we were) Tj T*
(unable to flex them forcibly.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The progressive weakness noted in the three cases has already been) Tj T*
(referred to, and may be a prodrome of paralysis, but there may be) Tj T*
(recovery without paralysis supervening.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Tremor.=--Tremor may be observed in a large number of cases of lead) Tj T*
(poisoning, and is invariably associated with paralysis, although the) Tj T*
(symptom of tremor by no means always progresses to definite palsy. Two) Tj T*
(types of tremor are described--fine and coarse--and Gübler further) Tj T*
(describes a type of tremor which is both rhythmic and intermittent. The) Tj T*
(tremor is usually distinctly increased on attempting to grasp or point) Tj T*
(the hand \(intention tremor\), but it is difficult to separate tremor) Tj T*
(from alcoholic tremor, and, further, it must not be forgotten that) Tj T*
(persons engaged in arduous work may show a certain amount of tremor due) Tj T*
(to muscular fatigue. Persistent tremor, however, is a symptom that is) Tj T*
(always to be noted and carefully watched.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Of the types of paralysis, the antibrachial is the most common, and,) Tj T*
(secondly, probably the brachial. The least common is the peroneal. The) Tj T*
(table on p. 54 shows the distribution of cases of paralysis, so far as) Tj T*
(they can be made out from reports received since 1904.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Closely associated with paralysis are affections involving the brain.) Tj T*
(Tanquerel, in his classical description of affections of the brain) Tj T*
(associated with lead poisoning, gives the following classification:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  1. Delirium.) Tj T*
(  2. Lead mania.) Tj T*
(  3. Psychic depression.) Tj T*
(  4. Coma.) Tj T*
(  5. Convulsions, saturnine eclampsia, or epilepsy.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is probably a very considerable relationship between insanity) Tj T*
(and lead poisoning, as pointed out by Robert Jones[17], the resident) Tj T*
(physician and Superintendent of Claybury Asylum.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
