() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Eye Changes.=--Two main forms of eye change are to be found amongst) Tj T*
(lead-workers. In the first place, temporary and sudden amaurosis makes) Tj T*
(its appearance, due no doubt to vascular change, either vaso-motor or) Tj T*
(hæmorrhagic. The trouble may occur in one or both eyes, may come on) Tj T*
(gradually, the patient merely being unable to distinguish letters or) Tj T*
(faces at a distance, or he may become suddenly totally blind. In the) Tj T*
(majority of cases the affection disappears under treatment, but in a) Tj T*
(small number of cases total blindness persists.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Occasionally nystagmus may be seen, but is not a common symptom;) Tj T*
(but dilatation of the pupils, quite apart from retinal changes, is) Tj T*
(not unusual. Inequality of the pupils may be observed, but partial) Tj T*
(dilatation of both pupils is more common, and is often associated with) Tj T*
(early anæmia. Conjunctival hæmorrhages are to be noted from time to) Tj T*
(time, without obvious cause, such as injury, etc., but in the majority) Tj T*
(of cases these have been associated with other symptoms.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first feature noticed in the eye is loss of brightness, and a) Tj T*
(curious lack-lustre of the eyes of persons intoxicated by lead is one) Tj T*
(of the general features making up the saturnine cachexia. Loss of) Tj T*
(brightness of the eye is associated in many other diseases with anæmia,) Tj T*
(but is particularly prominent in lead poisoning, much more so than is) Tj T*
(to be accounted for alone by the degree of blood-destruction, and is a) Tj T*
(point of which the examining surgeon should always take notice.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(One other form of eye change requires attention--namely, retinal) Tj T*
(changes due to circulatory disturbances. In an advanced case the whole) Tj T*
(picture is one of severe albuminuric retinitis, but in the earlier) Tj T*
(stages some engorgement of the vessels without alteration of the) Tj T*
(surrounding tissue is seen. Elschnig[21] associated this alteration in) Tj T*
(the vessels of the eye with vaso-motor changes caused by direct action) Tj T*
(of the poison, producing a vascular constriction or dilatation, and) Tj T*
(is inclined to regard the kidney disease frequently associated with) Tj T*
(this condition of the eye as something quite apart. He would regard) Tj T*
(the two affections as independent, and merely correlated through their) Tj T*
(common origin--lead intoxication. It has even been suggested by some) Tj T*
(observers that the change in the eye is secondary to cerebral ?dema.) Tj T*
(Thus Mannaberg[22] regards the encephalitis of saturnine origin as) Tj T*
(associated with chronic ?dema of the brain and spinal cord, which thus) Tj T*
(produces reflex irritation of the nervous system of the eye. Bikler[23]) Tj T*
