(and Weber[24] consider the symptoms as circulatory. From whatever) Tj T*
(cause the disease is set up, sooner or later changes in the form of) Tj T*
(obliterative arteritis take place, with gradual but ultimately complete) Tj T*
(loss of vision.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There are said to be no characteristic eye symptoms in acute cases) Tj T*
(of lead poisoning, whereas with chronic lead poisoning in many cases) Tj T*
(there is central and peripheral affection. The affections may be) Tj T*
(further divided into subjective and objective. Many of the subjective) Tj T*
(symptoms, such as loss of sight and blindness, are associated with) Tj T*
(definite eye lesions, which may be seen with the ophthalmoscope, but) Tj T*
(other definitely objective lesions may be present without any influence) Tj T*
(on sight to commence with. Folker[25] describes five cases of lead) Tj T*
(amblyopia in lead-workers from a pottery district, in all of which) Tj T*
(there was a peculiar symptom--the gradual failing of sight associated) Tj T*
(with colour flashes. When examined, the discs were described as white,) Tj T*
(and the vessels small.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Lockhart Gibson[26], in describing the cases of eye disease amongst) Tj T*
(the children in Queensland, found one symptom apparently in all the) Tj T*
(eyes examined--namely, great swelling of the discs. This swelling of) Tj T*
(the discs might be accompanied with no loss of sight whatever, and at) Tj T*
(other times had been accompanied with defective sight for many months) Tj T*
(previously. Some of the discs were excessively swollen. There were also) Tj T*
(to be seen patches of pigment and irregular swelling of the vessels,) Tj T*
(but no definite hæmorrhages. In the more acute cases, and particularly) Tj T*
(those associated with complete paralysis of the ocular muscles, total) Tj T*
(blindness usually followed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(As a rule, when complete amaurosis occurs in lead poisoning, blindness) Tj T*
(follows through double optic neuritis or neuro-retinitis, but amblyopia) Tj T*
(may be present without fundus changes. Occasionally the loss of) Tj T*
(sight may be regarded as of central origin. The renal disease so) Tj T*
(often associated with lead poisoning may cause the retinal changes) Tj T*
(accompanying it. An albuminuric neuro-retinitis may occur without) Tj T*
(albumin in the urine. As a rule, the eye of a lead-worker reacts to) Tj T*
(light and accommodation. Ophthalmoscopic examination may show very) Tj T*
(pink discs, patches of pigment scattered about irregularly outside) Tj T*
(the discs, with occasional definite hæmorrhage. The edge of the discs) Tj T*
(may show blurring, with further sclerosis and peri-arteritis of the) Tj T*
(vessels, a white sheath around the arteries being often visible. The) Tj T*
(neuritis on one or both sides may be associated with disturbances) Tj T*
(of sight, and diffusely red and cloudy papilla, with swelling or) Tj T*
(hæmorrhages. In choroidal atrophy pigmentation may also be seen.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Muscular System.=--One further point may be referred to in relation) Tj T*
(to the muscular system--namely, the occurrence of pain of a rheumatic) Tj T*
(type. Quite a number of cases of mild degrees of lead poisoning) Tj T*
(complain of arthralgic symptoms--that is to say, ?rheumatism.? Careful) Tj T*
(examination of such cases shows no evidence that the pain is a true) Tj T*
(arthralgia, neither does it seem to have a true relation to gout. The) Tj T*
