(pain as a rule is referable to the muscles themselves, and in such) Tj T*
(instances digital examination of the muscle in the region of the pain) Tj T*
(generally exhibits deep-seated tenderness. There does not appear to be) Tj T*
(any special marked tenderness along the trunks of the nerves supplying) Tj T*
(the muscle, nor is there evidence of hyperæsthesia of the skin. Such) Tj T*
(hyperæsthesia does occur in lead poisoning, but is generally associated) Tj T*
(with cerebral lesions. The pain, therefore, must be regarded rather as) Tj T*
(myalgic, and intercostal distribution of the pain is not infrequent;) Tj T*
(but although the symptom is one that is often complained of, it is) Tj T*
(an exceedingly difficult one to differentiate from other myalgias as) Tj T*
(a definite symptom of lead poisoning. The chief point in favour of) Tj T*
(the inclusion of this so-called lead rheumatism as a symptom of lead) Tj T*
(poisoning is the frequency with which it is noted in the reports by) Tj T*
(certifying surgeons. While, therefore, having no evidence to regard it) Tj T*
(as necessarily a definite symptom of poisoning, it is one which has) Tj T*
(been recorded in a considerable number of cases. As has been already) Tj T*
(suggested, there is some reason to think these myalgic pains may be due) Tj T*
(to minute hæmorrhages taking place in the muscles, thereby producing) Tj T*
(localized irritation to some extent comparable with the ?bends? of) Tj T*
(divers.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Post-Mortem Signs of Plumbism.=--Very real difficulty exists in) Tj T*
(determining from naked-eye appearances at a post-mortem examination) Tj T*
(whether the cause of death be due to chronic plumbism or not. The) Tj T*
(changes produced by several other forms of intoxication, notably) Tj T*
(chronic alcoholism, produce many of the same changes in the tissues as) Tj T*
(lead. Inspection of the organs in the case of plumbism can only give) Tj T*
(rise to a surmise that the cause of death is due to lead poisoning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There are, however, certain macroscopical appearances at an autopsy in) Tj T*
(the case of saturnism which should be carefully noted, and although) Tj T*
(alone they do not constitute sufficient evidence upon which to pass a) Tj T*
(definite opinion, they are still important as diagnostic signs in the) Tj T*
(light of histological and chemical examination.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Particular note should be paid at an autopsy of a case of suspected) Tj T*
(lead poisoning to the following points:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. Mouth, for the presence or absence of blue line, which, if present,) Tj T*
(must be examined with a lens.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. General condition of the abdominal viscera, and particularly of) Tj T*
(the mesenteric and perinephritic fat. In plumbism this is invariably) Tj T*
(reduced in quantity.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. The condition of the mesenteric vessels, as to whether or not they) Tj T*
(are engorged with blood, or whether or not leakage appears to have) Tj T*
(taken place.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. General condition of the arteries, for the presence of atheroma, etc.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
