(CHAPTER X) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CHEMICAL INVESTIGATIONS) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Very great assistance is afforded by chemical and histological) Tj T*
(diagnosis in the determination of cases of lead poisoning, especially) Tj T*
(when the case is likely to involve proceedings under the Workmen?s) Tj T*
(Compensation Act. In addition, a large amount of information is) Tj T*
(afforded to the certifying or appointed surgeon and the medical) Tj T*
(practitioner by adoption of certain easily carried out methods of) Tj T*
(diagnosis. It will be our purpose in the present chapter to describe) Tj T*
(as far as possible methods by which chemical or other investigation of) Tj T*
(a case of lead poisoning can be pursued, and the clinical methods of) Tj T*
(diagnosis which are applied in ordinary routine.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The majority of the methods described, especially the chemical) Tj T*
(investigation of material obtained from alleged fatal lead poisoning) Tj T*
(for the purpose of determining the presence or absence of lead,) Tj T*
(the histological examination of such tissues, and the examination) Tj T*
(quantitatively of excretions for lead, are processes which can only be) Tj T*
(carried out in a fully equipped laboratory, and certainly do not belong) Tj T*
(to the ordinary routine of medical work. The medical practitioner) Tj T*
(cannot be expected in the ordinary course of his routine work to) Tj T*
