(examine blood-films for basophile staining or to make differential) Tj T*
(blood-counts. Especially is this the case in the routine examination of) Tj T*
(large numbers of factory hands. Further, many of the processes involved) Tj T*
(in either the chemical or histological examination of the tissues) Tj T*
(require so much special apparatus, without which such work cannot be) Tj T*
(undertaken, that the mere cost of the necessary instruments precludes) Tj T*
(the investigation being carried on except in special laboratories. At) Tj T*
(the same time, our purpose is to point out how additional methods of) Tj T*
(research may be made use of in obscure cases, and how recourse should) Tj T*
(be had to a well-equipped laboratory in doubtful cases. Further,) Tj T*
(the coroner, when ordering a post-mortem examination, may ask for a) Tj T*
(histological and chemical examination.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Methods of Chemical Diagnosis.=--The presence of lead may require) Tj T*
(to be determined qualitatively and quantitatively, and the procedure) Tj T*
(may differ slightly as to which process it is necessary to adopt. The) Tj T*
(quantitative determination of the amount of lead present in organs or) Tj T*
(excretions of the body is of far more importance than the estimation) Tj T*
(or determination of the fact of its presence. We have already referred) Tj T*
(to the work of Gautier[1], who has found lead present in the tissue of) Tj T*
(normal persons with such constancy that French observers, at any rate,) Tj T*
(now speak of ?normal lead,? to distinguish it from lead which may be) Tj T*
(found in pathological conditions. There is, however, little doubt that) Tj T*
(the quantity of lead existing in the human body is exceedingly small.) Tj T*
(It is possible, with certain refined methods of chemical examination,) Tj T*
(that qualitative traces of this substance might be found. On the) Tj T*
(other hand, the methods of determination of lead are some of the most) Tj T*
(difficult in toxicological analyses, on account of the presence of) Tj T*
(other metals, particularly iron, which are exceedingly difficult to get) Tj T*
(rid of, and may easily lead to errors.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. =Qualitative Tests.=--The group reagent for lead is sulphuretted) Tj T*
(hydrogen in acid or alkaline solution. Lead is precipitated by this) Tj T*
(reagent as a black precipitate. Where no other metals are present, and) Tj T*
(where no organic matter is present at the same time, there is very) Tj T*
(little difficulty, and the usual method of the determination of lead in) Tj T*
(water by means of sulphuretted hydrogen is exceedingly easy.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Potassium iodide gives a yellow precipitate soluble on warming, and) Tj T*
(forms large crystals on the tube. Hydrochloric acid and chlorides give) Tj T*
(needle-shaped crystals, soluble in heat, and crystallizing out when) Tj T*
(cold. But the double chloride of potassium and lead is more soluble) Tj T*
(in heat, and still more soluble in cold, than is the pure chloride.) Tj T*
(This fact is made use of in the process to be described presently in) Tj T*
(separating lead from organic mixtures.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The direct examination is rarely possible or satisfactory, but the) Tj T*
(potassium cupric acetate method is one that may be applied to the) Tj T*
(qualitative estimation of lead in the tissues.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
