(  ?_On the Detection of Lead in Urine and Post-Mortem Specimens._--A) Tj T*
(  piece of kidney of 20 c.c. capacity was cut up into about a dozen) Tj T*
(  pieces. These were placed in an evaporating basin, and about 50 c.c.) Tj T*
(  of fuming nitric acid were poured into the dish. Dense brown fumes of) Tj T*
(  nitrogen oxides were evolved. When the action had subsided \(in from) Tj T*
(  two to three minutes\), the dish was placed upon a sheet of asbestos,) Tj T*
(  and allowed to simmer over the Bunsen flame for about an hour. If the) Tj T*
(  frothing appears in danger of running over the sides of the dish,) Tj T*
(  stirring with a glass rod or removal of the flame for a short time) Tj T*
(  may be necessary. Twenty-five c.c. of the fuming acid were added at) Tj T*
(  intervals of a quarter of an hour, and this process was repeated) Tj T*
(  three times. The destruction of the organic matter was so complete) Tj T*
(  that the whole of the piece of kidney passed into complete solution.) Tj T*
(  The solution was then evaporated down to a few c.c., neutralized) Tj T*
(  with caustic soda, filtered, and treated with hydrogen sulphide. A) Tj T*
(  dark-brown precipitate of lead sulphide was obtained. With potassium) Tj T*
(  chromate a yellow precipitate of lead chromate was obtained with) Tj T*
(  the same specimen of kidney which gave a negative result with the) Tj T*
(  KClO?-HCl method of destroying the organic matter. Our reagents,) Tj T*
(  dishes, etc., were then examined with a blank test, but we found no) Tj T*
(  lead.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  ?_Urine._--We then sought the presence of lead in the urine of) Tj T*
(  Cases 2, 3, and 4. Half a gallon of urine was evaporated down to) Tj T*
(  dryness in each of two basins. In one basin the residue was heated) Tj T*
(  until it was charred. Both residues were then treated separately) Tj T*
(  with fuming nitric acid, as just described. The uncharred residue) Tj T*
(  passed into solution, and on cooling deposited a white sediment. The) Tj T*
(  mother-liquor was neutralized and tested in the usual way. A brown) Tj T*
(  precipitate of lead sulphide was obtained in Case 2, while in Case) Tj T*
(  3 a well-marked black precipitate was obtained. The urine of Case 4) Tj T*
(  gave a negative result. The charred residue did not pass completely) Tj T*
(  into solution, and the tests for lead were not so well defined as) Tj T*
(  when the residue was uncharred. This shows that care must be taken to) Tj T*
(  prevent charring of the residue during evaporation.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
