(method of destroying organic matter\), any arsenic which may be present) Tj T*
(in the hot solution is not given off with its acid aqueous vapour,) Tj T*
(arsenious chloride dissolved in hydrochloric acid being volatile) Tj T*
(only when the solvent is concentrated. Any possibility of loss may) Tj T*
(be avoided, however, by furnishing the flask, in which the organic) Tj T*
(matter is being destroyed, with a condenser and receiver. The second) Tj T*
(objection, as far as lead is concerned, is met by taking care to filter) Tj T*
(the solution whilst hot; if only a limited amount of lead is present,) Tj T*
(it then remains in solution as chloride so long as the liquid is hot,) Tj T*
(and will consequently pass through the filter. A considerable quantity) Tj T*
(is kept in solution in the cold, as it forms a combination with) Tj T*
(potassium chloride, which is more soluble than lead chloride alone. If) Tj T*
(a large amount is present, it will not all be found in the filtrate;) Tj T*
(the substance left on the filter, therefore, must always be tested for) Tj T*
(lead. In toxicological work, however, the amount of lead present is not) Tj T*
(as a rule more than will remain dissolved in the cold. Silver chloride,) Tj T*
(being insoluble either in hot or cold water, will not pass through the) Tj T*
(filter; consequently the salts of silver require dealing with in a) Tj T*
(special manner.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Dry Method._--This is effected by heating the finely divided substance) Tj T*
(to redness, so that it is either carbonized or completely incinerated.) Tj T*
(When cold, the residue is drenched with nitric acid, and sufficient) Tj T*
(heat is afterwards applied to drive off the free acid. The nitrate) Tj T*
(of the metal is then dissolved in water, filtered, and dealt with) Tj T*
(according to the kind of metal present.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The dry method is unsuitable in the case of the more volatile metals,) Tj T*
(as arsenic, antimony, and, in a lesser degree, lead, tin, and zinc.) Tj T*
