(=The Anæmia of Lead Poisoning.=--As has been pointed out in Chapter) Tj T*
(VIII \(p. 135\), the anæmia of lead poisoning is one due to the) Tj T*
(destruction of the red blood-cells. This is evidenced not only) Tj T*
(by the curious sallow complexion, by the occasional presence of) Tj T*
(hæmatoporphyrin in the fæces and urine, and often by the curious) Tj T*
(yellow of the sclerotics, but also by an increase in the viscosity of) Tj T*
(the blood itself. Moreover, the urine of persons suffering from lead) Tj T*
(poisoning is invariably highly coloured, and may even show the presence) Tj T*
(of methæmoglobin. As the anæmia is generally a symptom of continued) Tj T*
(lead absorption for a long period, and does not necessarily occur with) Tj T*
(every case of colic--in fact, acute colic may often supervene without) Tj T*
(any symptoms of continued anæmia--the persons suffering from lead) Tj T*
(anæmia should be removed from their direct contact with the dangerous) Tj T*
(processes, and should be given, if possible, work in the open air.) Tj T*
(Iron and arsenic may be used, preferably in combination, whilst the) Tj T*
(iodide of iron often gives good results. Whatever preparation of iron) Tj T*
(is given, care should always be exercised in avoiding any constipating) Tj T*
(effect, and the free action of the bowel should be maintained, together) Tj T*
(with a liberal supply of milk. Potassium iodide may be also given.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
