(=Amount of Lead Fume and Dust in the Atmosphere Breathed.=--Lead fuses) Tj T*
(at 325° C. and boils at between 1450° and 1,600° C. It is volatile when) Tj T*
(heated to a cherry-red colour--about 550° C.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Experiments[A] carried out in the laboratory of a lead smelting works) Tj T*
(in London to determine the temperature at which leady fumes rise) Tj T*
(from the surface of open baths of molten lead, showed that unless) Tj T*
(pure lead is heated to about 500° C., and at the same time stirred,) Tj T*
(no appreciable fume comes off, and that from lead, at the same) Tj T*
(temperature, under ordinary working conditions, little or no lead) Tj T*
(in the form of oxide passes into the air. From lead that has been) Tj T*
(unrefined or which contains zinc--that is, lead in the earlier stages) Tj T*
(of its manufacture \(in the reverberatory furnace\)--leady fume was not) Tj T*
(given off at temperatures less than 760° C. even when stirred, because) Tj T*
(at a temperature of 600° C. the surface of the molten metal became) Tj T*
(covered with fluid slag, which will not allow any oxide to be given) Tj T*
(off. Impurities such as tin or antimony prevent the oxidation of molten) Tj T*
(lead at lower temperatures, and give it a bright, shiny colour. When) Tj T*
(heated to about 600° C., these impurities form a slag on the surface) Tj T*
(of the lead containing antimoniates and stannates of lead, which do) Tj T*
(not evolve lead fumes unless heated to temperatures never likely to be) Tj T*
(reached in open lead pots. The reason why molten refined lead can give) Tj T*
(off lead fume more readily than those named is because the oxide formed) Tj T*
(on the surface is a dry powder and not in the form of slag. Hence, when) Tj T*
(the bath is stirred, some of the dry oxide is broken up and may rise) Tj T*
(into the air. When a bath of molten lead is not stirred at all, it can) Tj T*
(be heated to over 740° C. without finding oxide in the air aspirated--a) Tj T*
(temperature not obtained under ordinary working conditions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  [A] In these experiments air was aspirated through an iron funnel) Tj T*
(  having an area of 113 square inches \(12 inches diameter\), placed at) Tj T*
(  a height of 1¹?? inches above the molten metal, and connected to an) Tj T*
(  iron tube 3 feet in length and ¹?? inch in diameter. Inside the iron) Tj T*
(  tube was a glass tube, one end reaching own to the top of the funnel) Tj T*
(  and the other connected with a tube containing pure loose asbestos) Tj T*
(  wool, and continued down to a tightly stoppered bottle holding) Tj T*
