(metal tubing\), determined the temperature of the various pots and baths) Tj T*
(of molten lead used in the Sheffield district. As was anticipated,) Tj T*
(temporary cessation of work, stirring up of metal, recoking of) Tj T*
(furnaces, and other causes, produced fluctuations of temperatures from) Tj T*
(minute to minute in the same pot, and in its different parts. The) Tj T*
(compensated pyrometer used gave for file-hardening pots a maximum of) Tj T*
(850° C., and a minimum of 760° C., the average mean working temperature) Tj T*
(being about 800° C. The variations of temperature of lead used for) Tj T*
(tempering tangs of files and rasps was found to be high, and largely) Tj T*
(unrestricted from a practical standpoint. The maximum was 735° C.,) Tj T*
(and the minimum 520° C., the average mean working temperature being) Tj T*
(650° to 700° C., varying more than this within a few hours in the same) Tj T*
(pot. Spring tempering is carried out at some comparatively constant) Tj T*
(temperature between a maximum of nearly 600° C. and a minimum of 410°) Tj T*
(C., depending on the kind of steel and the purpose for which the steel) Tj T*
(is to be employed. Generally, the temperature required rises as the) Tj T*
(percentage of carbon in the steel is diminished. As these baths are) Tj T*
(larger than file-hardening pots, the temperature range is higher at) Tj T*
(the bottom than at the top unless well stirred up. Some lead pots are) Tj T*
(set in one side of a flue, and the temperature in the mass is then) Tj T*
