(Similar experiments were carried out in letterpress printing works. The) Tj T*
(average temperature was 370° C. in the stereo pots, and in the linotype) Tj T*
(pots at work 303° C. Scrap lead melting-pots when hottest registered) Tj T*
(424° C., but registered as low as 310° C., according to the amount of) Tj T*
(scrap added, the state of the fire underneath, etc. The best practical) Tj T*
(working temperature depends largely on the composition of the metal) Tj T*
(used. That at some factories is the same for stereo drums as for lino) Tj T*
(pots--viz., 81·6 per cent. lead, 16·3 per cent. antimony, and 2·0 per) Tj T*
(cent. tin, added to harden the lead. On the other hand, some printers) Tj T*
(use a higher percentage of antimony in the lino than in the stereo) Tj T*
(metal. Lead melts at 325° C., and antimony at 630° C., but by adding) Tj T*
(antimony to lead up to 14 per cent. the melting-point is reduced at) Tj T*
(an almost uniform rate to 247° C., after which further addition of) Tj T*
(antimony raises the melting-point. This explains why temperatures as) Tj T*
(low as 290° C. are practicable for linotype pots. The molten eutectic) Tj T*
(has a specific gravity of about 10·5, whereas the cubic crystals) Tj T*
(average 6·5 only; therefore in these pots the latter float on the top,) Tj T*
(and excess of antimony is to be expected in the skimmings or on the) Tj T*
(surface.) Tj T*
