(=Dust.=--Reference to the table shows that the conditions in the) Tj T*
(pottery workrooms, as stated in Column 7, are reflected in Columns 3) Tj T*
(and 5. Further details from his experiments may be useful. Thus, in a) Tj T*
(dipping room where low-solubility glaze was in use, the amount of lead) Tj T*
(in the dust collected per 10 cubic metres of air was 0·70 milligramme.) Tj T*
(The average of four experiments where there were no dipping boards) Tj T*
(was 1·80 milligrammes, and where dipping boards were used, 3·75;) Tj T*
(_i.e._, 1·95 milligrammes of lead in the dust per 10 cubic metres of) Tj T*
(air is added by the use of dirty dipping boards. As the result of his) Tj T*
(experiments, Duckering believes that approximately 1·95 milligrammes of) Tj T*
(lead per 10 cubic metres of air was due to the fine spray given off in) Tj T*
(the shaking of the ware. In bright sunlight, he says, the spray can be) Tj T*
(seen dancing high above the dipping tub. In a dipping house where work) Tj T*
(was done slowly by two occupants only, the proportion of lead in the) Tj T*
(measured quantity of air was also low--0·58 milligramme per 10 cubic) Tj T*
(metres. Where, in the absence of special provision made for admission) Tj T*
(of fresh air to a fan, the air was drawn from a neighbouring room in) Tj T*
(which lead processes were carried on, the amount of lead rose to 5·76) Tj T*
(milligrammes at the level breathed by the gatherer at a mangle. In) Tj T*
(ware-cleaning the average of all his observations where lead was used) Tj T*
(\(eleven\) was 3·44 milligrammes; and he concluded that ?wet cleaning of) Tj T*
(ware causes less direct contamination of the atmosphere, even where) Tj T*
(no local exhaust is applied. A still more important result of wet) Tj T*
(cleaning, however, is that the overalls keep much freer of dust.? The) Tj T*
(highest results were obtained when the process of ware-cleaning was) Tj T*
(done outside the influence of the exhaust draught. In one instance,) Tj T*
(where the ware was cleaned at a distance of 6 feet from the exhaust) Tj T*
(opening, 13·34 milligrammes per 10 cubic metres of air were found.) Tj T*
(Subsequently at the same point, after the exhaust system of ventilation) Tj T*
(had been remodelled, 0·95 milligramme only was present. Even in a) Tj T*
(stillage room in which no work was done other than the placing on and) Tj T*
(removal of the boards from the racks, the lead content per 10 cubic) Tj T*
(metres of the air was 1·08 milligrammes. In glost-placing, the average) Tj T*
(of four experiments was 1·83 milligrammes--no doubt the result of glaze) Tj T*
(on the boards. As much as 9·11 milligrammes of lead was found per 10) Tj T*
(cubic metres of air in the centre of a large majolica-painting room,) Tj T*
(with wooden floors and much traffic in it. Wooden floors generally) Tj T*
(appeared to influence the results, as determinations of the lead) Tj T*
(present were higher in rooms with them than with tiled floors.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In coach-painting the proportion of lead found by Duckering in the air) Tj T*
(breathed during the actual time of sand-papering explains the severe) Tj T*
(incidence of poisoning in this class of work. The table shows the) Tj T*
(amount of lead in the air to be enormous, and in many cases much in) Tj T*
(excess of the amount found in the air when wiping off in the tinning) Tj T*
(of hollow-ware. The work of sand-papering is, however, very rarely) Tj T*
(continuous, the time occupied in it being, for the painter, about one) Tj T*
(to two hours daily; for the brush hand, two to three and a half hours;) Tj T*
