(and for the painter?s labourer, four to five hours.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Knowing intimately the processes at which the estimations recorded) Tj T*
(in the table were made, the relative frequency of cases of plumbism) Tj T*
(reported among those employed at them, and the duration of employment) Tj T*
(prior to attack, we believe that, if the amount of lead present in the) Tj T*
(air breathed contains less than 5 milligrammes per 10 cubic metres of) Tj T*
(air, cases of encephalopathy and paralysis would never, and cases of) Tj T*
(colic very rarely, occur. And this figure is a quite practical one in) Tj T*
(any process amenable to locally-applied exhaust ventilation. Somewhere) Tj T*
(about 2 milligrammes, or 0·002 gramme, of lead we regard as the lowest) Tj T*
(daily dose which, inhaled as fume or dust in the air, may, in the) Tj T*
(course of years, set up chronic plumbism.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Local Exhaust Ventilation.=--In considering preventive measures) Tj T*
(against lead poisoning, precedence must be given to removal of fumes) Tj T*
(and dust by locally-applied exhaust ventilation, as, unfortunately, the) Tj T*
(wearing of a respirator is neither in itself a sufficient protection,) Tj T*
(nor, if it were, could the constant wearing of one be enforced. A) Tj T*
(respirator is of no use against lead fume. In the case of dust, the) Tj T*
(conditions which it must fulfil to be effective are, first, that the) Tj T*
(air breathed is freed from dust, and, secondly, that it should not) Tj T*
(incommode the wearer. Further, it should be simple in construction,) Tj T*
(easily applied, and allow of frequent renewal of the filtering medium.) Tj T*
(No existing respirator of moderate price conforms quite satisfactorily) Tj T*
(with these requirements. The more closely to the face it is made to) Tj T*
(fit, and the more effectually the air is filtered, the greater is the) Tj T*
(inconvenience experienced when it is worn. This inconvenience is due to) Tj T*
(the exertion \(showing itself in increase of the respiratory movements) Tj T*
(and pulse-rate\) caused in aspirating the air through the filtering) Tj T*
(medium, and rebreathing some portion of the expired breath, containing) Tj T*
(a much greater proportion of carbonic acid gas and of moisture at) Tj T*
(a higher temperature than are present in fresh air. Respirators,) Tj T*
(therefore, except for work lasting a short time--half an hour to) Tj T*
(an hour--cannot be considered an effective or sufficient means of) Tj T*
(protecting the worker against dust. If a respirator must be worn, the) Tj T*
(simplest form is a pad of ordinary non-absorbent cotton-wool \(absorbent) Tj T*
(wool quickly becomes sodden and impervious\), about 3 inches by 4) Tj T*
(inches, placed over the mouth and nostrils, and kept in position by) Tj T*
(elastic bands passed round the ears. The pad should be burnt after use.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(With a smooth, impervious floor, however, and ventilation designed to) Tj T*
(remove the fumes and dust at, or as near as possible to, the point of) Tj T*
(origin, lead poisoning would become very rare in most of the industries) Tj T*
(to be described. The essential points of such a system are--\(1\) The) Tj T*
(draught or current of air set in motion either by heat or by a fan; \(2\)) Tj T*
(the ducts along which the current travels; \(3\) the hoods or air-guides) Tj T*
(designed to intercept and catch the fumes and dust at the point of) Tj T*
(generation; \(4\) inlets from the outside air into the room to replace) Tj T*
(continuously the air extracted, and, in many cases, \(5\) a suitable dust) Tj T*
(filter or collector.) Tj T*
