(_Exhaust by Heat._--Processes giving rise to fumes or to dust liberated) Tj T*
(on stirring or skimming, which can be dealt with by the draught created) Tj T*
(in the furnace flue or over a bath of molten metal provided with) Tj T*
(adequate hood and duct up which the heated air travels, are--Smelting,) Tj T*
(refining, spelter manufacture, and the numerous operations) Tj T*
(necessitating the melting of lead, such as tinning with a mixture of) Tj T*
(tin and lead, sheet lead and lead piping, stereo pots in letterpress) Tj T*
(printing, pattern-making, tempering springs, file-hardening, etc. The) Tj T*
(dusting of red-hot metallic surfaces, as in vitreous enamelling, might) Tj T*
(possibly also be dealt with in the same way. The disadvantage of the) Tj T*
(exhaust by heat is the uncertainty and inequality of the draught, and) Tj T*
(the size of the duct necessary to cope with the volume of rarefied air) Tj T*
(from above the molten vessel.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The closer the hood is brought down over the point where the fumes) Tj T*
(escape, the less risk is there of cross-currents deflecting them into) Tj T*
(the workroom. Hence all baths of molten metal should have the sides) Tj T*
(and back closed in, leaving as small a space open in front as is) Tj T*
(practicable in view of necessary skimming or other operations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the case of tinning baths, Duckering[4] describes completely) Tj T*
(successful results when from the top of the hood a shaft at least 24) Tj T*
(inches in diameter was carried vertically upwards into the open air to) Tj T*
(a height of 18 feet, and the top of the shaft fitted with a wind screen) Tj T*
(in the form of a very large cone, having its lower edge below the upper) Tj T*
(edge of the shaft, and its nearest point at least 8 inches from the top) Tj T*
(of the shaft. Smoke produced in large quantity at any point 6 inches) Tj T*
(outside the front of the hood was entirely drawn into it. As, however,) Tj T*
(the inrush of air caused an eddy of the fumes at the upper edge of) Tj T*
(the opening, the edges of the hood were turned inwards, so that the) Tj T*
(operation of wiping was done in a sort of short tunnel. In general, it) Tj T*
(may be said that the diameter of pipes leading from hoods to the outer) Tj T*
(air \(on the efficacy of the draught in which success depends\) is much) Tj T*
(too small. Frequently mere increase in size will convert an indifferent) Tj T*
(draught into a good one. The height of the hood also--_i.e._, the) Tj T*
(distance between its lower border and the point where it joints) Tj T*
(the duct--is of importance. The shorter this distance is, the less) Tj T*
(serviceable does it become for the removal of fume. Indeed, it may even) Tj T*
(retain the fume which, were the hood not present, would rise to the) Tj T*
(roof. Sometimes safety is increased by making the hood double, leaving) Tj T*
(a space between the two sheets, and so concentrating the draught at) Tj T*
(the centre and at the margin. With a fan, ducts of less diameter can) Tj T*
(be used than when dependence is placed on heat alone. A duct carried) Tj T*
(into a chimney-stack has the advantage of dispersing the fume at a safe) Tj T*
(distance from the workroom.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The variableness of the draught produced by heat makes it unsuitable) Tj T*
(for removal of dust, except such as arises from skimming. The) Tj T*
(receptacle for the skimmings should always be kept inside the canopy of) Tj T*
(the hood. We have, however, seen the dust given off in the heading of) Tj T*
