(_Hoods and Air-Guides._--As the object of hoods is to concentrate the) Tj T*
(draught on the fumes or dust to be removed from the worker, position) Tj T*
(in regard to origin of the fumes or dust requires first consideration.) Tj T*
(The more restricted the opening consistent with unimpeded work, the) Tj T*
(more effective is the draught, and the less disturbed will it be by) Tj T*
(cross-currents in the workroom. Pendock lays it down as a useful) Tj T*
(principle that the area of the front opening into the hood should not) Tj T*
(be more than four times that of the exhaust throat--_i.e._, the point) Tj T*
(of junction of the hood and duct \(Fig. 4\). Not less important is it) Tj T*
(that the draught should operate below the breathing level. Preference) Tj T*
(as to the direction to be given to the exhaust current should be in the) Tj T*
(order named: \(1\) Downwards; \(2\) downwards and backwards combined; \(3\)) Tj T*
(backwards and upwards combined; and \(4\) upwards only. Use should be) Tj T*
(made, for the removal of the fumes or dust, of any initial current of) Tj T*
(hot air set up from a bath of molten metal or from a heated metallic) Tj T*
(surface, as in vitreous enamelling. Hence under such circumstances only) Tj T*
(\(3\) and \(4\) need be considered. Generally hoods applied err in having) Tj T*
(too wide an opening, or they are placed too far away from the source of) Tj T*
(danger. They require sometimes to be adjustable to suit different-sized) Tj T*
(articles. Care is necessary to see that, when a hood has been adjusted) Tj T*
(for large articles, it is readjusted for smaller-sized articles. The) Tj T*
(principle of ventilation downwards and backwards is recognized as right) Tj T*
(for grinding and polishing on a wheel, since the tangential current set) Tj T*
(up by the wheel in its rotation is utilized. Pug-mills in paint-works) Tj T*
(are perhaps best ventilated by applying the exhaust to a dome-shaped) Tj T*
(hood covering the posterior half of the mill. Edge-runners must be) Tj T*
(encased, with an exhaust pipe attached to the casing and sliding) Tj T*
(doors or shutters for introduction or removal of material \(Fig. 5\). A) Tj T*
(small negative pressure inside the casing is all that is necessary,) Tj T*
(so as to insure passage of air inwards and not outwards. Branch ducts) Tj T*
(must protect the casks out of which material is scooped, and the) Tj T*
(receptacle into which it is discharged. In scooping out dry colour) Tj T*
(from a barrel, it is unwise to attempt to remove the dust created at) Tj T*
(every displacement of air on removal of a scoopful by means of a hood) Tj T*
(suspended over the barrel. Instead, the last joint of the duct should) Tj T*
(be a telescopic one, so that it can be lowered into the barrel, and be) Tj T*
(kept at a distance of about 6 inches above the material. The air is) Tj T*
(thus drawn downwards into the barrel \(Fig. 6\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Illustration: FIG. 4 shows a well-designed arrangement of hoods,) Tj T*
(duct, and fan, in the packing of white lead, and the filter-bags for) Tj T*
(collecting the dust so removed. An additional safeguard is introduced,) Tj T*
(as the casks stand upon grids through which a down-draught is) Tj T*
(maintained by connecting the space underneath with the exhaust system.) Tj T*
(\(Drawing supplied by the Sturtevant Engineering Company, Limited,) Tj T*
