(An appointed time for the surgeon?s attendance at the factory has been) Tj T*
(found necessary, because, in conformity with the literal wording of the) Tj T*
(regulation, the occupier should not continue to employ a worker who,) Tj T*
(for one reason or another, has not been examined by the surgeon during) Tj T*
(the prescribed interval. With knowledge of the date and hour posted) Tj T*
(in a conspicuous place in the factory, excuse for absence becomes) Tj T*
(difficult. Alteration by the surgeon of his appointed time should,) Tj T*
(whenever possible, be given beforehand. Surgeons in the past frequently) Tj T*
(made examination of the persons employed with the view of taking them) Tj T*
(unawares, and so of precluding special preparation beforehand--a) Tj T*
(practice which had its advantages; but they are outweighed by the) Tj T*
(hardship inflicted on workers who were unavoidably absent, as, for) Tj T*
(example, night-workers. A health register is supplied to all occupiers) Tj T*
(where periodical medical examination is enjoined, the headings of which) Tj T*
(and manner of entry are indicated later on in this chapter.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The objects which the surgeon should have in mind in making his) Tj T*
(examination are:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(1. To prevent lead poisoning and minimize lead absorption.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. To obtain information for the occupier and Inspector of Factories of) Tj T*
(the relative danger of one process and another with a view to adoption) Tj T*
(of remedial measures.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In safeguarding the health of the workers, he should make effort to) Tj T*
(gain their confidence, in order to be able to attach proper value to) Tj T*
(statement as to subjective symptoms. Suspicion in their minds that the) Tj T*
(examination is made solely in the interests of the employer militates) Tj T*
(against success, and increases inclination to conceal symptoms and) Tj T*
(to give untruthful answers as to the state of health since the last) Tj T*
(examination. In our opinion, the surgeon will best carry out the first) Tj T*
(object by attention to the second. The study of thousands of reports) Tj T*
(on cases of lead poisoning convinces us that 90 per cent. at least are) Tj T*
(due to inhalation of dust and fumes. The surgeon, therefore, should) Tj T*
(utilize the earliest sign of lead absorption to warn the occupier and) Tj T*
(inspector of conditions favourable to the development of plumbism, and) Tj T*
(due probably either to some unguarded spot in the manufacturing process) Tj T*
(whereby dust or fumes are not being removed completely, or to ignorance) Tj T*
(or carelessness \(often excusable in the absence of proper instruction\)) Tj T*
(on the part of the worker. He should direct, therefore, especial) Tj T*
(attention to new workers, not only because of their need for guidance) Tj T*
(as to precautions to be observed and greater liability to attack) Tj T*
(during the first year of employment, but also because development of) Tj T*
(signs in them constitutes the surest guide to defects in the process) Tj T*
(of manufacture. Occasionally symptoms in a worker may be so menacing) Tj T*
(as to demand immediate suspension, but generally before the power is) Tj T*
(exercised attempt to rectify the condition which gives rise to them) Tj T*
(should be made. The surgeon can do much by influencing the foremen and) Tj T*
(forewomen, who will necessarily come before him for examination, in) Tj T*
(insisting on the supervision by them of care and cleanliness by the) Tj T*
(workpeople under their charge. Should suspension, despite attention in) Tj T*
ET
