(lead-worker, the face, apparently anæmic, flushes directly.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(2. The brightness of the eyes, state of the pupils, and condition of) Tj T*
(the conjunctiva and of the ocular muscles.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(3. The mouth should next be examined, and search made for any evidence) Tj T*
(of blue line around the gum.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(4. The gait should be watched both on advancing to, and retiring from,) Tj T*
(the surgeon. If necessary, the man should be made to walk a few steps.) Tj T*
(Although the peroneal type of palsy is extremely rare, the possibility) Tj T*
(of its occurrence should never be absent from the mind of the surgeon.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(5. The man should then be directed to stretch his hands out in front) Tj T*
(of him, with wrists extended and fingers widely spread. Presence) Tj T*
(or absence of tremor should be looked for, and the condition of the) Tj T*
(finger-nails, as to the practice of biting, etc. The extensor power) Tj T*
(should then be tested, firstly of the fingers. While the hands of the) Tj T*
(workman remain outstretched, the surgeon places the forefinger of his) Tj T*
(hand in the outstretched palm of the workman, and the ball of the) Tj T*
(thumb upon the extreme tip of each finger, and by gently pulling it) Tj T*
(down, noting the spring present in the muscles. This test is probably) Tj T*
(the most delicate there is for detection of early extensor paralysis.) Tj T*
(The condition of the lumbricals and interossei are noted on movement) Tj T*
(of the fingers. The extensors of the wrist are then further examined,) Tj T*
(the workman being directed to flex his arm at the elbow and strongly) Tj T*
(pronate the wrist, so that the palm of the hand is directed forwards.) Tj T*
(He is then told to close the fist when the surgeon endeavours to flex) Tj T*
(the wrist, the workman at the same time resisting by forcible extension) Tj T*
(of his wrist. Ordinarily the extensor communis digitorum and minimi) Tj T*
(digiti are sufficiently powerful to resist a very powerful pull upon) Tj T*
(the wrist; and if the wrist is found to yield, it is a sign that the) Tj T*
(muscles are affected. Sometimes the strength of the wrists and fingers) Tj T*
(is judged by the surgeon placing his palms on the dorsum of the) Tj T*
(patient?s outstretched hands, and seeing whether the patient can be) Tj T*
(prevented from lifting them without flexing the wrists or finger-joints.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The test detects \(1\) paralysis which has been recovered from to a large) Tj T*
(extent; \(2\) commencing partial paralysis; and \(3\) weakness of muscular) Tj T*
(power, especially in those who have worked in lead for a number of) Tj T*
(years. This weakness appears to be an effect of lead upon the muscular) Tj T*
(tissue or dependent on debility, the result of lead absorption, and) Tj T*
(independent of nerve implication. We have known the condition to remain) Tj T*
(unaltered for years, and also to undergo alteration, being at times) Tj T*
(absent for months together. Occasionally reports of definite paralysis) Tj T*
(refer to pre-existing weakness.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(6. The pulse is next noted. The pulse-rate need not ordinarily be) Tj T*
(counted, but if it is either very slow or fast careful examination at) Tj T*
(the conclusion of the general inspection should be made.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is well to make all these points before asking any questions. After) Tj T*
