(Some degree of pallor is so commonly met with in adolescence that it) Tj T*
(is the progressive development of the anæmia which the surgeon must) Tj T*
(especially watch for. As a danger signal, therefore, it has the same) Tj T*
(significance nearly as the blue line; but when lead absorption has) Tj T*
(affected the elements in the blood, progressive anæmia in new workers,) Tj T*
(attributable to the employment, and showing no tendency to improve) Tj T*
(after watching for a few months, is an indication for suspension or) Tj T*
(transference to other work. In older workers, with a duration of) Tj T*
(employment of five years or more, there may be a quasi-pathognomonic) Tj T*
(pallor which does not vary from year to year. In them it must be) Tj T*
(supposed that an equilibrium has been established, and development of) Tj T*
(other symptoms, such as tremor, wrist weakness, or albuminuria, becomes) Tj T*
(significant. Attention has already been directed to the distinct) Tj T*
(saturnine facies associated with anæmia, and characterized by loss of) Tj T*
(fat, particularly noticeable in the orbit and buccinator region of the) Tj T*
(face. ?So far as the question of any worker?s suspension is concerned,?) Tj T*
(says Dr. King Alcock, ?I prefer to make my instinctive _primâ facie_) Tj T*
(distrust of a saturnine pallor the basis for action. The pallor of) Tj T*
(plumbism cannot be summed up in hæmoglobin and corpuscular content;) Tj T*
(it is the expression of a complex toxæmia resulting from defective) Tj T*
(assimilation and excretion?[3].) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The knowledge the surgeon should gain of the idiosyncrasies of the) Tj T*
(workers by his periodical examination will enable him to appraise at) Tj T*
(their proper value the nature and degree of the symptoms in notified) Tj T*
(cases.) Tj T*
