() Tj T*
(General sense of propriety--and it is on this ground, and not from) Tj T*
(danger attaching to non-observance, that we press it--suggests that) Tj T*
(overalls should be kept apart from working clothes, and preferably) Tj T*
(outside any room in which a lead process is carried on. So long as) Tj T*
(actual contact between the two sets of garments is prohibited, we) Tj T*
(do not see objection to the same cloakroom sufficing for both. The) Tj T*
(best arrangement that we have seen is a room in which each worker) Tj T*
(has two lockers--one for the storage of overalls, and the other for) Tj T*
(clothing put off during working hours. This presupposes supervision) Tj T*
(and effective discipline. We think that all reasonable need in the) Tj T*
(provision to be made is met by numbered pegs on one side of the room) Tj T*
(or wide passage for clothing, and on the other pegs correspondingly) Tj T*
(numbered for the overalls. Means for heating and drying the clothes) Tj T*
(should not be overlooked \(see Fig. 8\).) Tj T*
