() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Separation of Processes.=--Lead processes should, as far as possible,) Tj T*
(be carried on in such a way that there is aerial disconnection of one) Tj T*
(process from another. Separate workrooms, although desirable unless) Tj T*
(very small, may not always be possible, but by exhaust ventilation) Tj T*
(processes may be arranged so that dust is not a source of danger to) Tj T*
(persons working elsewhere. Instances could be given where, in paint and) Tj T*
(colour works, persons employed in the grinding of earth colours, in) Tj T*
(which no lead entered, succumbed to attacks of plumbism from contiguity) Tj T*
(of the plant with that for grinding lead colours. Threading up of) Tj T*
(castor runners on a wire previous to dipping used to be done, prior to) Tj T*
(1901, in the dipping-house itself, with the result that some severe) Tj T*
(cases of poisoning occurred. By the rules of 1901 the process was) Tj T*
(required to be done in a place separate from any place where dipping) Tj T*
(\(or other scheduled process\) was carried on, and very few cases have) Tj T*
(occurred since. And if exhaust ventilation is relied on, Duckering?s) Tj T*
(experiments \(p. 206\) show the attention to detail necessary to prevent) Tj T*
(air charged with lead dust being drawn to the spot by the fan. The need) Tj T*
(for separation of processes becomes less in proportion as the cubic) Tj T*
(space of the room increases, and dust and fumes are locally removed.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Age of Employment.=--The same considerations as have been stated) Tj T*
(above govern this point also. Wherever lead dust or fumes arise,) Tj T*
(whether exhaust ventilation is applied or not, persons of either sex) Tj T*
(under eighteen years of age are probably rather more susceptible to) Tj T*
(attack by reason of natural failure to appreciate the risk run. When) Tj T*
(periodical medical examination in addition to exhaust ventilation has) Tj T*
(been adopted, the age limit can safely be reduced to sixteen. Where) Tj T*
