(  Danger is greatest from breathing leady dust or fume, but eating with) Tj T*
(  unwashed hands, and biting nails or putting such things as sweets or) Tj T*
(  pipes into the mouth while the fingers are soiled with lead, all help) Tj T*
(  to cause poisoning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Many workers in lead have a blue line round the edge of the gums,) Tj T*
(  but the first actual symptoms of the injurious action of lead on the) Tj T*
(  system are costiveness, colicky pains in the stomach, headache, and) Tj T*
(  marked paleness. Occasionally headache is associated with epileptic) Tj T*
(  fits, a very serious condition, which may be followed by loss of) Tj T*
(  sight. ?Wrist-drop,? loss of power in the muscles moving the fingers) Tj T*
(  and wrist, results sometimes from lead poisoning, and may cause) Tj T*
(  permanent disablement from work. It does not usually come on until) Tj T*
(  after a person has worked in lead for some years.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Lead, even if absorbed in small quantities, has a tendency to remain) Tj T*
(  in the system, and if care be not taken it will go on accumulating,) Tj T*
(  so that in time the health may become permanently damaged, even) Tj T*
(  without any definite attack recognized as lead poisoning.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Girls and boys, because they are not so likely to observe the) Tj T*
(  necessary precautions as grown-up people, should be closely watched.) Tj T*
(  Women should be especially careful, as the injurious effect of) Tj T*
(  lead in them may seriously interfere with the healthiness of their) Tj T*
(  children.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  As lead does not enter the system through the pores of the skin, it) Tj T*
(  can in great measure be avoided by--) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  1. Taking special care to avoid raising dust. It is to the interest) Tj T*
(  of everyone to see that ventilating arrangements are in order for) Tj T*
(  carrying dust away at the point where it is produced.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Any little cloud constantly made at work is sure, if breathed, to set) Tj T*
(  up lead poisoning. Where lead colours are used wet, danger arises) Tj T*
(  from the splashing of the material and its subsequent drying into) Tj T*
(  dust.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  2. Paying scrupulous attention to cleanliness of the hands, face,) Tj T*
(  teeth, and clothing. The hands and nails should always be cleaned) Tj T*
(  with soap and nail-brush before food is eaten, and it is a wise) Tj T*
(  practice also to wash out the mouth. The teeth should be brushed at) Tj T*
(  least once a day, preferably before the evening meal.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  3. Never commencing work on an empty stomach. Food containing fat,) Tj T*
(  such as bacon and milk, is suitable.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Overall suits, if worn, should never be shaken to rid them of dust.) Tj T*
(  They require washing at least once a week.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Aperient medicine, such as Epsom salts \(one or two teaspoonfuls) Tj T*
(  in water\), can be taken once or twice a week with advantage by) Tj T*
