() Tj T*
(When galena is treated in a blast furnace, preliminary roasting is) Tj T*
(indispensable, and in many smelting works its treatment takes place in) Tj T*
(a reverberatory or open-hearth furnace, and not in a blast furnace.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The three principal methods applicable to extraction of lead from ores) Tj T*
(are--\(1\) The roast and reaction method; \(2\) the roast and reduction) Tj T*
(method; and \(3\) the precipitation process.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(By the _roast and reaction method_ a part of the galena is first) Tj T*
(converted into oxide and sulphate of lead with access of air.) Tj T*
(Subsequently, on shutting off the air-supply and increasing the) Tj T*
(temperature, a reaction takes place. The sulphur in the unchanged) Tj T*
(sulphide combines with the oxygen of the oxide and sulphate to form) Tj T*
(sulphur dioxide, which is carried away by the draught into the bricked) Tj T*
(flue, leaving metallic lead behind. The process is carried on in a) Tj T*
(reverberatory or open-hearth furnace.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the _roast and reduction method_ the first portion of the process) Tj T*
(is carried out in a reverberatory furnace, the galena being roasted) Tj T*
(pretty completely to lead oxide and sulphate, which are then--usually) Tj T*
(in a blast furnace--reduced to the metallic state with coke and other) Tj T*
(reducing agents, such as iron.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(By the _precipitation process_ galena was decomposed at a high) Tj T*
(temperature by means of metallic iron, forming a mixture of iron and) Tj T*
(lead sulphide. This method was only applicable to rich lead ores, and) Tj T*
(is now given up.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The three methods are hardly ever independent of one another, as the) Tj T*
(rich slag or residues, for instance, which are obtained by the first) Tj T*
(method are retreated by the second, and the second is, as has been) Tj T*
(stated, almost always combined with the first.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On tapping the blast or reverberatory furnace, the lead is drawn) Tj T*
(off into a lead well or sump, from which, when cool, it is ladled) Tj T*
(into moulds, while the slag is run into movable metal pots or along) Tj T*
(specially-prepared channels. The slag run off from the reverberatory) Tj T*
(furnace contains much lead locked up as silicate, which requires to be) Tj T*
(retreated, usually in the blast furnace. During the roasting process) Tj T*
(much raking of the material is necessary. The slag from the blast) Tj T*
(furnace should contain less than 1 per cent. of lead.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On the Continent and in America, the Huntingdon-Heberlein process has) Tj T*
(been extensively adopted, with lessened incidence of poisoning, the) Tj T*
(result of mechanical methods of working, obviating hand labour, and the) Tj T*
(low temperature \(diminishing risk from lead fume\) at which the roasting) Tj T*
(is carried on. In this process the crushed ore is desulphurized by) Tj T*
(first mixing with lime and heating in presence of air in a revolving) Tj T*
(furnace, provided with automatic rabble, at moderate temperature \(about) Tj T*
(700° C.\). Subsequently the roasted material is conveyed from closed) Tj T*
(bins, into which it falls automatically, by dust-proof elevators to) Tj T*
