(broken by hand, and charged with coke in the usual way into the blast) Tj T*
(furnace.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In some lead-smelting works the material arrives on the premises) Tj T*
(in the form of ingots of base bullion--_i.e._, impure lead rich in) Tj T*
(silver--the product of previous smelting of the ore where it is mined) Tj T*
(in Australia or Spain. And one of the main objects of the blast-furnace) Tj T*
(smelting of galena in the factory is to produce a base bullion rich) Tj T*
(in precious metals. The lead so obtained requires further softening) Tj T*
(or refining to get rid of copper, antimony, arsenic, and tin. This) Tj T*
(is effected in a reverberatory furnace, first at a low temperature) Tj T*
(to allow of formation of furnace dross, which is removed through) Tj T*
(the working doors, and secondly with increase of heat and access of) Tj T*
(air to oxidize, in the order named, the tin, arsenic, and antimony.) Tj T*
(Finally the lead is tapped into kettles or pots. If free from silver,) Tj T*
(such lead, when poured into moulds, is ready for the market; but if) Tj T*
(rich in silver, it is treated for the recovery of that metal either) Tj T*
(by \(_a_\) Pattinson?s process, depending on the higher temperature of) Tj T*
(crystallization of lead than of an alloy of lead and silver, which) Tj T*
(enables a separation of one from the other to be made by a process) Tj T*
(of ladling the crystalline from the liquid portion; or, much more) Tj T*
(commonly, by \(_b_\) Parkes?s process, depending on the formation,) Tj T*
(on addition of zinc to a pot of molten lead, of crusts consisting) Tj T*
(of an alloy of silver, lead, and zinc. The crusts obtained in the) Tj T*
(latter process, after cooling, are broken up, placed in a crucible,) Tj T*
(and the zinc driven off at a temperature of 1,000° C. in a dezincing) Tj T*
(Faber du Faur retort. The rich bullion, retained either in the last) Tj T*
(kettle by the Pattinson process, or remaining in the crucible after) Tj T*
(dezincing, next undergoes cupellation--_i.e._, exposure to a blast) Tj T*
(of air in a furnace. The lead is oxidized into litharge, which drops) Tj T*
(into a receptacle below the furnace, leaving the silver behind. In all) Tj T*
(lead-smelting works the draught from the furnace carries much dust of) Tj T*
(ore and fuel, and fume, consisting of sulphide, sulphate, and oxides of) Tj T*
(lead, into the flues. The dust is easily collected in dust chambers,) Tj T*
(but the fume requires ducts of great length--sometimes a mile or) Tj T*
(more--in which to deposit.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Dangers and Prevention._--The risk from dust in general labouring) Tj T*
(work, in depositing the ores in bins, in removing them to, and charging) Tj T*
(them into, the furnace, can only be controlled by watering, preferably) Tj T*
(by a spray. From the blast furnace lead fume and carbon monoxide may) Tj T*
(escape at the point where charging is done, if there is back pressure) Tj T*
(from blockage in the flues, or if the furnace blast is not working) Tj T*
(perfectly. In tapping the lead and in manipulations such as charging,) Tj T*
