(Lead absorption among the men actually employed in the Pattinson) Tj T*
(and Parkes?s processes is comparatively rare, as the temperature of) Tj T*
(the molten metal does not exceed 450° to 500° C. When, however, the) Tj T*
(zinc-silver-lead and gold alloy is removed for treatment in special) Tj T*
(furnaces for distillation off of the zinc, prior to cupellation, the) Tj T*
(lead from the Parkes?s pot, now free from silver, but containing traces) Tj T*
(of zinc, antimony, and other impurities, is run in some works into) Tj T*
(what are termed ?market pots? for a final refining. Air and steam are) Tj T*
(blown through to oxidize the impurities. The pot is skimmed twice,) Tj T*
(the first dross containing antimony, etc., and the second a fine dust) Tj T*
(consisting of lead \(60 per cent.\) and zinc. The risk of poisoning at) Tj T*
(this point is considerable, although an exhaust fan connects up the) Tj T*
(cover of the pot with a cyclone separator, to carry away the fume when) Tj T*
(the steam is blown through. In other works this dezincing is done in) Tj T*
(a refining furnace, the material being then in a slaggy state, thus) Tj T*
(hindering development of fumes. After the condensation of the zinc in) Tj T*
(the distillation of the silver-lead and zinc crust the cover of the) Tj T*
(pot is raised, and the remaining metal, containing 80 per cent. of) Tj T*
(lead at a temperature of about 2,000° F., is ladled out into moulds) Tj T*
(for treatment in the cupelling furnace. The temperature at which this) Tj T*
(ladling operation has to be done makes the work impossible for those) Tj T*
(unaccustomed to it. Exhaust ventilation in the operation of emptying) Tj T*
(the pot, and cutting off the heat by a water-cooled jacket, suggest) Tj T*
(themselves as means to combat the undoubted risk.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In cupellation the temperature is high \(about 2,000° C.\), and fume will) Tj T*
(escape from the working door and from the opening where the rich lead) Tj T*
(is fed into the furnace. The danger here is sufficiently recognized) Tj T*
(by hoods and ducts placed in front of the furnace, but the draught,) Tj T*
(unless the ducts are connected up with a high-pressure fan, may prove) Tj T*
(inadequate to carry away all the fume.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Flue-cleaning, carried out usually at quarterly or half-yearly periods,) Tj T*
(is dusty work, as much of the dust is in so fine a state of division as) Tj T*
(to repel contact with water.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Smelting of other metals when the ores contain appreciable amounts) Tj T*
(of lead is equally productive of plumbism. Thus, in the year 1901) Tj T*
(fourteen cases were reported from an iron works for the manufacture of) Tj T*
(spiegeleisen, the ore \(now no longer used\) coming from Greece[2]. In) Tj T*
(previous years it would appear to have been even greater. A remarkable) Tj T*
(feature of all the reported cases from this factory was that the form) Tj T*
