(_Chamber Process._--In this method, almost universally used in Germany,) Tj T*
(and adopted in at least one large white lead works in this country,) Tj T*
(a chamber arranged with numerous sets of parallel bars on which the) Tj T*
(thin strips of lead are set saddle-wise takes the place of the stack) Tj T*
(in the Dutch process. Carbonic acid gas and acetic acid vapour act) Tj T*
(on and corrode the strips. In a period of from eight to ten weeks) Tj T*
(the corrosions mostly fall to the ground. Such of the strips as do) Tj T*
(not fall have to be lifted off the bars, having been previously well) Tj T*
(saturated with water from a hosepipe, and are dropped on to the floor) Tj T*
(of the chamber. We are not satisfied that working in the dark, confined) Tj T*
(chamber by artificial light is less dangerous than working on the) Tj T*
(stacks. Chamber-made lead undergoes practically the same subsequent) Tj T*
(processes as have been described.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
