() Tj T*
(The stringent Special Rules for the White Lead Industry show) Tj T*
(what other precautions, in addition to exhaust ventilation, are) Tj T*
(necessary--especially personal cleanliness. The effect of one other) Tj T*
(factor--casual labour--however, must be referred to. The condition at) Tj T*
(the present time is very different from that which existed twelve years) Tj T*
(ago. From one factory in 1899, depending much on casual labour, 111) Tj T*
(cases were reported, and from another 72. In an inquiry made by one of) Tj T*
(us in 1898, information was obtained of the actual number employed on) Tj T*
(any one date, and of the total number passing through the factories in) Tj T*
(a year.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Among the firms with regular employment at that time, the incidence of) Tj T*
(lead poisoning was 60 per 1,000 on the average number employed, and in) Tj T*
(those with casual employment 390 per 1,000. Work in lead had secured a) Tj T*
(bad name, and no one who could get employment elsewhere would take to) Tj T*
(it. Consequently, the class of men applying for work was a low one--men) Tj T*
(discharged from other employment and those unfitted for skilled labour.) Tj T*
(Not a few were addicted to alcohol. The work was unskilled, and had the) Tj T*
(additional advantage to men of that class that much of it was piece) Tj T*
(work, paid at a good rate, which could be finished as a rule by three) Tj T*
(o?clock in the afternoon.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Diminution in the number of cases from 399 in 1899 to 34 in 1910 has) Tj T*
(been brought about mainly by--\(1\) Improved structural conditions;) Tj T*
(\(2\) adoption of mechanical means \(cranes, rails, hoists, etc.\) for) Tj T*
(conveyance of material in substitution for hand carrying; \(3\) exhaust) Tj T*
(ventilation, where dust arises as in packing and paint-mixing; \(4\)) Tj T*
(periodical medical examination; \(5\) diminution in height of the stoves) Tj T*
(or adoption of mechanical drying stoves; \(6\) conversion of white lead) Tj T*
(into paint by means of direct mixture with oil while in the pulp) Tj T*
(stage; and \(7\) substitution of small, square, glazed pots--cockney) Tj T*
(pots--requiring the lead strips to be placed on them, for the deep) Tj T*
(castle pots into which the lead grids are folded in the white beds.) Tj T*
(Prohibition of female employment in the dangerous processes was made) Tj T*
(prior to the Special Rules of 1899. Their greater susceptibility as) Tj T*
(compared with men, the special effect of lead on the uterine functions,) Tj T*
(and the unsuitability of much of the work for women, fully justified) Tj T*
(the step recommended by the White Lead Committee in 1898.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Earthenware and China[22].=--The industry includes the manufacture of) Tj T*
(earthenware, china, tiles, majolica ware, Rockingham ware \(teapots\),) Tj T*
(sanitary ware, china furniture, and electrical fittings, and any other) Tj T*
(articles made from clay; but of the total 6,865 persons employed in) Tj T*
(lead processes in the whole of the United Kingdom in 1907, 5,834) Tj T*
(are included in the manufacture of the first three. And even in the) Tj T*
(manufacture of earthenware, china, and tiles, the poisoning which) Tj T*
(occurs is not distributed evenly over the whole of the factories.) Tj T*
