(_Dangers and Prevention._--In casting, danger is mainly from dust in) Tj T*
(depositing the skimmings, and from fume also when old accumulator) Tj T*
(plates are melted down. For these reasons exhaust ventilation over the) Tj T*
(melting pots should be provided, embracing also \(by branch ducts if) Tj T*
(necessary\) the receptacles into which the lead ashes are thrown. In) Tj T*
(mixing and pasting, the danger is from dust of oxides of lead to be) Tj T*
(controlled \(see Fig. 6\) by--\(1\) Exhaust ventilation by branch ducts) Tj T*
(protecting \(_a_\) the barrel from which the material is scooped, \(_b_\)) Tj T*
(the mechanical mixer into which the weighed quantity of oxide is) Tj T*
(discharged, \(_c_\) the bench at which the mixing by hand is done; \(2\)) Tj T*
(dampness of benches and floor to prevent raising of dust either by) Tj T*
(manipulation of the \(often\) heavy plates or trampling into powder the) Tj T*
(paste which may fall on the ground.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In assembling or putting together of the formed plates, and in earlier) Tj T*
(stages of the manufacture also, filing or use of a wire brush causes) Tj T*
(production of metallic lead dust and of the oxides when the brush) Tj T*
(touches them--a danger only to be met by exhaust ventilation. How far) Tj T*
(the poisoning to which the lead burners engaged in assembling plates) Tj T*
(is attributable to lead fume, produced by the high temperature of the) Tj T*
(blowpipe flame, and how far to handling \(with inevitable dislodgment of) Tj T*
(dust\) has not been satisfactorily settled. Incidence of poisoning on) Tj T*
(this class of worker in the past has been marked.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Generally there is need for impervious floors, solidly built, so as) Tj T*
(to prevent vibration and the raising of dust from passage of trolleys) Tj T*
(conveying the heavy plates. Gloves are frequently provided, more to) Tj T*
(protect the hands from contact with the sulphuric acid used in making) Tj T*
(the paste and jagged edges of the plates than as a preventive of lead) Tj T*
(absorption.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the 10 years 1900-1909 incidence, according to precise occupation,) Tj T*
(has been--Casting, 33; pasting, 114; lead burning, 69; and assembling) Tj T*
(the plates, etc., 69.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Glass-Cutting[31].=--Red lead enters largely into the mixture of raw) Tj T*
(materials for the manufacture of glass. Flint glass, for instance,) Tj T*
(contains 43 per cent. of lead. The raw materials \(white sand, red) Tj T*
(lead, and generally saltpetre\) require to be very carefully mixed, and) Tj T*
(a few cases of poisoning have been reported from the dust raised in) Tj T*
(sieving. One man works the sieve, resting on two runners across the) Tj T*
(bin, while another shovels the mixture into the sieve. The operation is) Tj T*
(not a continuous one, and respirators have principally been relied on) Tj T*
(to protect the workers. It should be possible to carry out the mixing) Tj T*
(operations in a dust-tight closed apparatus.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Poisoning from lead fumes generated in a glass furnace is unknown. Lead) Tj T*
