(Although rouge and oxide of iron have replaced putty powder to some) Tj T*
(extent--especially for the polishing of the bevelled edges of plate) Tj T*
(glass--no substitute can at present be found to give the final lustre) Tj T*
(and brilliancy required in the case of cut glass and in certain kinds) Tj T*
(of high-class work, such as polishing lenses.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Locally applied exhaust ventilation has robbed the process of its) Tj T*
(dangers. Pyramidal-shaped hoods enclose the spindle and putty box and) Tj T*
(brush before which the workman sits. The draught of the fan prevents) Tj T*
(escape of spray. The lad who feeds the brush with putty powder stands) Tj T*
(at the side, and in our experience his cap and clothes are now free) Tj T*
(from signs of splashing. Formerly the polishing was done by each man) Tj T*
(at his own berth, thus endangering the health of all working in the) Tj T*
(vicinity, as the custom of the trade is that the same man carries) Tj T*
(through the work both of cutting and polishing. Polishing occupies only) Tj T*
(about a fifth of a man?s time, and it has now, owing to the position of) Tj T*
(the fan, to be carried out in one particular part of the room.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  Dr. D?Arcy Ellis[32], Certifying Surgeon for the Stourbridge) Tj T*
(  district, has described the processes as formerly carried out:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  ?The mixture of lead and tin is heated over a bright fire in a) Tj T*
(  shallow iron pan. As it melts, the top scum which forms is skimmed) Tj T*
(  off, dried, pounded to a powder in an iron mortar, and afterwards) Tj T*
(  sieved. The person who does this work always suffers more or less.) Tj T*
(  He usually protects himself by wearing a respirator--there is a good) Tj T*
(  draught at the flue, and the sieve is enclosed in a box--but there) Tj T*
(  is always a certain amount of dust. This putty-powder is used on the) Tj T*
(  wooden wheel, and is dabbed on the wheel as it revolves. All good) Tj T*
(  bold work can be polished in this way, and there is not much risk) Tj T*
(  to the workman, as the speed at which the wheel revolves causes the) Tj T*
(  mixture to cling and not fly about. This process does not answer) Tj T*
(  for any fine work, so it is contended; and to enable this kind of) Tj T*
(  work to be properly polished brushes made of bristles are used. They) Tj T*
(  are mounted on an iron spindle, and are usually about 6 inches to 7) Tj T*
(  inches in diameter, with a face of 1 inch to 1¹?? inches broad. They) Tj T*
(  are driven at a speed of about 2,000 revolutions a minute. The putty) Tj T*
(  powder is applied to these brushes \(which are of various sizes\) in) Tj T*
(  the same way as to the wooden wheel--that is, by dabbing it on. For) Tj T*
(  smaller work, such as tumblers and wine-glasses, the workman applies) Tj T*
(  the putty mixture himself, holding the glass against the brush with) Tj T*
(  his right hand, and using his left underneath to apply the mixture.) Tj T*
(  Where, however, larger work has to be done in which the workman) Tj T*
(  cannot manage with one hand, the service of a boy is called in, who) Tj T*
(  does what is called the ?feeding up.? This boy stands partly in front) Tj T*
(  and partly at the side of the brush, and applies the mixture with one) Tj T*
(  hand with the wisp of straw. In this position the boy gets splashed) Tj T*
