(Of the 697 cases included in returns during the ten years 1900-1909,) Tj T*
(352 were reported from railway carriage and waggon works, 299 from) Tj T*
(ordinary carriage works and wheelwrights? shops, and 46 \(separate) Tj T*
(tabulation was only commenced in 1905\) in motor-car works. In the year) Tj T*
(1903 inquiry was made in 603 factories and workshops, including all) Tj T*
(classes of coach and carriage building, railway carriage and engine) Tj T*
(works, and agricultural implement works. Information was asked \(among) Tj T*
(other things\) as to--\(1\) The number of persons employed in painting) Tj T*
(with lead paints; \(2\) description of the method adopted for smoothing) Tj T*
(the coats of paint; and \(3\) the substitutes tried for white-lead) Tj T*
(paint. Persons employed numbered 9,608. In 52 factories and workshops) Tj T*
(smoothing of the coats of paint was not practised, while in the) Tj T*
(remaining 551 it was affirmed that a wet method alone \(pumicestone) Tj T*
(and water\) was used in 178, a dry method alone \(sandpaper\) in 39, and) Tj T*
(both wet and dry methods at some stage or other of the work in 334.) Tj T*
(Substitutes were mentioned as having been tried in 94 instances, but) Tj T*
(this was almost exclusively for filling and jointing, and not for the) Tj T*
(first or priming coat.) Tj T*
