(The figure 178 \(wet method alone\) is probably much too high, because,) Tj T*
(while it is true that pumicestone and water alone are used for the) Tj T*
(flat surfaces of the body--the bulk of the work--dry sandpapering of) Tj T*
(the first two priming coats and of the final finishing coats \(when) Tj T*
(of white, cream, or yellow colour\), of the under parts of carriages,) Tj T*
(iron chassis of motor-cars, and of curved surfaces, such as the spokes) Tj T*
(of wheels, is almost universal. The reason for thus treating the) Tj T*
(priming coat dry is that a wet process would raise the grain of the) Tj T*
(wood. The 52 factories in which it was stated that no smoothing was) Tj T*
(done were nearly all premises for the repair or manufacture of railway) Tj T*
(trucks, requiring no special finish, and the 39 factories in which) Tj T*
(only sandpaper was said to be used in smoothing were premises in which) Tj T*
(rough, cheap, or common vehicles, such as carts, were made. Use of) Tj T*
(sandpaper is quicker and less expensive than use of pumicestone, and) Tj T*
(water and wet methods cannot be used very well on iron surfaces.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In ordinary coach and carriage painting, after the sandpapering of) Tj T*
(the first two priming coats, six or seven coats of ?filling? \(usually) Tj T*
(ground slate mixed with gold size and turpentine\) are applied, and) Tj T*
(each coat is rubbed down wet. Joints and interstices of woodwork and) Tj T*
(irregularities in iron surfaces are generally filled in with a stopping) Tj T*
(or paste of white lead, in the smoothing of which sandpaper is used.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the manufacture of motor-cars, the terne \(lead\) coated sheets which) Tj T*
(form the body, after preliminary preparation, receive two coats of a) Tj T*
(lead paint. These are either lightly sandpapered or ?flatted? with) Tj T*
(pumice and water. Three coats of non-poisonous filling follow, and are) Tj T*
(flatted with pumice or German brick and water. The body then passes to) Tj T*
(a skilled workman, who applies the final coats of colour. For facing) Tj T*
(mouldings and for corners throughout all stages of the processes, dry) Tj T*
(sandpaper takes the place of pumice and water. All stopping on the) Tj T*
(chassis, the first lead coat on the bonnet, and all coats of paint on) Tj T*
(the wheels, are sandpapered dry. Sometimes a third of a man?s time may) Tj T*
(be taken up in sandpapering alone.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(_Dangers and Prevention._--Grave risk of inhaling lead dust is present) Tj T*
(\(see the table on p. 47\) when sandpaper is used, often at a point just) Tj T*
(above the mouth and nostrils. Rubbing down the wheels is perhaps the) Tj T*
(most dangerous work, and for this exhaust ventilation can be applied) Tj T*
(locally. Inventive genius has yet to be directed to some modification) Tj T*
(of the vacuum-cleaning apparatus, so that an exhaust can be attached to) Tj T*
(the back of the worker?s hand or in connection with a frame in which) Tj T*
(the sandpaper is held. In the process of wet rubbing, the abraded) Tj T*
(coats drip on to the floor, and when dry may rise as dust into the) Tj T*
(atmosphere.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Precisely similar operations, or only modified in detail, have) Tj T*
(accounted for heavy incidence of lead poisoning in the painting of) Tj T*
(perambulators, of safes, of bicycles, of bedsteads, of gas-meters,) Tj T*
(the ?metallic? enamelling of baths \(in which also chipping off of) Tj T*
