(such workshop conditions. Despite the limited extent to which the Act) Tj T*
(applies to lead poisoning of house-painters and plumbers, seeing that) Tj T*
(it is industrial in origin many practitioners notify cases, with the) Tj T*
(result that the number every year exceeds considerably that from any) Tj T*
(other lead industry in the country. Thus, the number notified in the) Tj T*
(ten years 1900-1909 was 1,973, including 383 deaths. The proportion) Tj T*
(of deaths to persons notified is much higher than for lead industries) Tj T*
(generally \(19·4 per cent., as compared with 4·0 per cent.\). If the) Tj T*
(proportion of cases to deaths were the same in house-painting as in) Tj T*
(other industries \(and it is a fair assumption to make\), the number of) Tj T*
(cases would be 9,418.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(When investigation is made into the reported cases, the pre-dominance) Tj T*
(of the severer symptoms--paralysis, brain symptoms, and chronic) Tj T*
(plumbism--is brought out. Causation of poisoning, in order of) Tj T*
(importance, appears to be: \(1\) Dust from sandpapering one surface of) Tj T*
(paint before applying another; \(2\) dust from mixing dry white lead) Tj T*
(with oil; \(3\) dust arising from paint that has dried on overalls; \(4\)) Tj T*
(contamination of food with unwashed hands; and \(5\) fumes from burning) Tj T*
(off old paint.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(=Use of Leadless Paints.=--Opinion still differs as to the feasibility) Tj T*
(of substituting zinc sulphide or zinc oxide \(or a combination of the) Tj T*
(two\) for white lead in paints, in spite of elaborate investigation of) Tj T*
(the point by commissions of inquiry appointed notably by the French,) Tj T*
(Austrian, and Dutch Governments. There is, however, general consensus) Tj T*
(of opinion that for the painting of internal surfaces of houses and) Tj T*
(of all surfaces which are not exposed to the weather zinc paints have) Tj T*
(the advantage \(apart from their non-poisonous quality\) over white-lead) Tj T*
(paint of not changing colour. The technique for applying zinc oxide) Tj T*
(paint differs much from that for applying white lead. Being much less) Tj T*
(dense, it requires to be ground with a greater proportion of oil, and) Tj T*
(the vehicles and driers necessary for the thinning of the stiff paste) Tj T*
(are different from those ordinarily used for thinning and mixing white) Tj T*
(lead. Coats of zinc oxide should be applied as thin as possible, and) Tj T*
(hence there is the drawback that where three coats of white lead will) Tj T*
(suffice, four coats of zinc oxide may be necessary unless the paint) Tj T*
(is skilfully applied. The best method of applying zinc oxide paint) Tj T*
(with the brush has to be learnt in order to get the best effect. The) Tj T*
(ordinary house-painter, therefore, accustomed to the use of lead paint,) Tj T*
(cannot expect to obtain the same result from zinc paint treated in the) Tj T*
(same way. And zinc oxides differ in value as pigments according to the) Tj T*
(methods of production. That obtained by direct roasting of the ore) Tj T*
(\(franklinite and zincite\) is superior to that prepared by the indirect) Tj T*
(method of oxidation of spelter.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Zinc sulphide enters into the composition of many white paints mixed) Tj T*
(with zinc oxide, barytes, and often lead sulphate. Its defect in) Tj T*
(colour is thus concealed, and it adds to the mixture the important) Tj T*
(property known to the painter as ?body.? Under a variety of names,) Tj T*
(such as ?Orr?s enamel white,? ?patent zinc white,? and ?lithopone,?) Tj T*
