(At the present time committees appointed by the Home Office are) Tj T*
(inquiring into the coach-painting and house-painting industries in this) Tj T*
(country.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The results of careful and detailed experiments made by the White Lead) Tj T*
(Commission appointed by the Dutch Government, which inquired into the) Tj T*
(subject, are summarized as follows:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  I. Zinc-white paints are much better able to withstand the action of) Tj T*
(  sulphuretted hydrogen gas than white-lead paints.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  II. Zinc-white paints do not withstand the action of sulphurous acid) Tj T*
(  in the atmosphere as well as white-lead paints.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  As this gas is present in coal-smoke of locomotives, steamers, tall) Tj T*
(  chimneys, etc., zinc-white paint much exposed to such smoke--for) Tj T*
(  instance, in railway-stations, etc.--will soon become corroded, and) Tj T*
(  cannot then replace white lead.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  III. Zinc-white paints applied on zinc, Portland cement, or iron \(the) Tj T*
(  latter having previously been provided with first coats of red oxide) Tj T*
(  of lead or iron\), are able to withstand the action of the open air) Tj T*
(  for a space of five years quite as well as white-lead paints, and can) Tj T*
(  entirely replace the latter, provided they are not exposed to the) Tj T*
(  action of vapours containing sulphurous acid.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  IV. In the interior of buildings zinc-white paints, applied on wood,) Tj T*
(  iron, zinc, Portland cement, and plaster, are as good as white-lead) Tj T*
(  paints; and can entirely replace the latter, provided they are not) Tj T*
(  exposed much to vapours containing sulphurous acid or to much damp.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  V. Zinc-white paints applied on wood, if not exposed much to the) Tj T*
(  action of sulphurous acid gas, will in many cases last during) Tj T*
(  five years in the open air as well as white-lead paints, and can) Tj T*
(  replace the latter with good results. But in all places where water) Tj T*
(  accumulates, as on window-sills, the lower side of cornice-work,) Tj T*
(  etc., they will, even after three or four years, deteriorate to such) Tj T*
(  a degree that repainting will become necessary for the preservation) Tj T*
(  of the wood; in this respect, therefore, they are inferior to) Tj T*
(  white-lead paints.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  VI. Zinc-white paints, such as the White Lead Commission have used) Tj T*
(  successfully, cover at least equally as well as the white-lead paints) Tj T*
(  customary in this country.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  The zinc-white putty used by the White Lead Commission is quite as) Tj T*
(  serviceable as ordinary white-lead putty.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(  VII. Painting with zinc-white paint, such as the Commission used on) Tj T*
(  new woodwork in the open air, does not cost more than painting with) Tj T*
(  the white-lead paints customary for that purpose.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
