(chromate of lead to the yarn more securely.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Proof of the greater danger from orange chrome is found in the fact) Tj T*
(that Dupré was able to wash 1 pound of dust \(0·29 per cent.\) from 345) Tj T*
(pounds of heavy orange yarn, and only 1 pound \(0·03 per cent.\) could be) Tj T*
(washed from 3,300 pounds of light yellow or green yarn.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In none of the factories were the workers engaged solely on the) Tj T*
(dangerous yellow and orange chrome-dyed yarn. In some the work may) Tj T*
(last an hour or two every day, in others for an hour or two every day) Tj T*
(in alternate weeks, or for one week in every three or four weeks, and) Tj T*
(perhaps in a dozen factories the work may not be done more frequently) Tj T*
(than half a day a month, or even one in three months.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Particular attention was paid to the nature of the exhaust ventilation) Tj T*
(at the ?heading? posts, as this is the most important point in the) Tj T*
(protection of the workers. It was provided in eight out of the nine) Tj T*
(principal yarn-dyeing factories. The exception was one where the work) Tj T*
(was said to be solely for the home market. In one a 2 foot 6 inch) Tj T*
(Blackman fan was placed in the wall without connection of the ?heading?) Tj T*
(posts with it by means of ducts and hoods. In four, hoods and ducts) Tj T*
(of wood, square in section, with right-angle bends, had been locally) Tj T*
(applied to the posts. In other four, hoods and ducts were of metal,) Tj T*
(circular in section. The velocities in feet per minute \(obtained with) Tj T*
(a Davis self-timing anemometer\) were taken at the opening into the) Tj T*
(branch duct behind or under the post. The value of anemometric tests in) Tj T*
(detecting blockages or interference in the ducts is evident from the) Tj T*
(table on p. 300.) Tj T*
